<node id="&quot;《计算机网络释疑与习题解答》&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"This is a supplementary textbook authored by Xie Xiren that provides detailed solutions to exercises, mentioned as a separate publication for readers seeking in-depth problem-solving guidance."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-243d8875e135092451e82e6ee7250806</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 9}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 41}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;RFC 文档&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"RFC (Request for Comments) documents are referenced as key standards and technical references updated throughout the textbook chapters, representing ongoing internet standardization activities."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-243d8875e135092451e82e6ee7250806</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 9}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 41}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;LTE&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"LTE (Long-Term Evolution) is a specific mobile communication technology discussed in Chapter 9 of the textbook, forming part of the content revised with input from experts like Ding Guodong."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-243d8875e135092451e82e6ee7250806</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 9}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 40}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;蜂窝移动通信网&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Cellular mobile communication networks were previously covered in earlier editions but removed in this revision; they represent a class of telecommunication events/systems relevant to networking education."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-243d8875e135092451e82e6ee7250806</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;互联网&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"GEO"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The Internet (互联网) is referenced as a global network infrastructure that connects systems worldwide and serves as the foundation for various communication protocols and services discussed in the text."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Internet is a central topic of the textbook, described as a 'network of networks' with evolving infrastructure and protocols, forming the core subject matter of the educational material."&lt;SEP&gt;"The internet is treated as a global-scale networked environment where data flows, congestion occurs, and throughput is influenced by traffic distribution; in the absence of a more precise 'network' type, it is categorized as a geographic-like entity representing a digital space."&lt;SEP&gt;"互联网 is described as the world's largest computer network, originating in the United States and now spanning globally. It provides services based on connectivity and resource sharing, enabling communication, commerce, education, and more. It functions as a critical societal infrastructure."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-561c228fa7944971c082eed5e584dc98&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-243d8875e135092451e82e6ee7250806&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-41cb6db802a71038c261f08ac9a7f0ca&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0174bb3213f0a9872650fbd9b001dae3</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 9}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 39}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;智能手机&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Smartphones are highlighted as the primary user devices connecting to the Internet, marking a significant shift in network usage patterns addressed in Chapter 9."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-243d8875e135092451e82e6ee7250806</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 9}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 39}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;ADSL&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"TECHNOLOGY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Asymmetric Digital Subscriber Line (ADSL) is a broadband access technology that enables high-speed data transmission over traditional telephone lines."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-0d86bcb46f981cf5384a0d47ea9a60d4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;HFC NETWORK&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"TECHNOLOGY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Hybrid Fiber-Coaxial (HFC) Network is a broadband access infrastructure combining optical fiber and coaxial cable to deliver high-bandwidth services such as internet and television."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-0d86bcb46f981cf5384a0d47ea9a60d4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;FTTX&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"TECHNOLOGY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"FTTx (Fiber to the x) refers to a family of broadband network architectures that use optical fiber to replace all or part of the copper local loop used in traditional telecommunications."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-0d86bcb46f981cf5384a0d47ea9a60d4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;PPP&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"TECHNOLOGY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Point-to-Point Protocol (PPP) is a data link layer protocol used for direct communication between two nodes, commonly employed in dial-up and broadband connections."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-0d86bcb46f981cf5384a0d47ea9a60d4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;CSMA/CD&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"TECHNOLOGY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Carrier Sense Multiple Access with Collision Detection (CSMA/CD) is a media access control protocol used in early Ethernet networks to manage data transmission and detect collisions."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-0d86bcb46f981cf5384a0d47ea9a60d4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;ETHERNET&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"TECHNOLOGY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Ethernet is a family of networking technologies commonly used in local area networks (LANs), referenced here with a 1 Gbit/s capacity example."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ethernet is a widely used family of networking technologies for local area networks (LANs), defined by IEEE 802.3 standards, supporting various speeds including 10 Mbps, 100 Mbps, 1 Gbps, and beyond."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ethernet is referenced as the data link layer technology used for transmitting framed data with specific header and trailer overheads."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-b44f82ed51f5dd17e63648288cc859c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2ab20554e89073ba513e052dbf5667c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d86bcb46f981cf5384a0d47ea9a60d4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;VIRTUAL LAN (VLAN)&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"TECHNOLOGY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Virtual Local Area Network (VLAN) is a logical segmentation of a physical network at the data link layer, allowing improved traffic management, security, and scalability."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-0d86bcb46f981cf5384a0d47ea9a60d4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;100BASE-T&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"TECHNOLOGY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"100BASE-T is a Fast Ethernet standard operating at 100 Mbps over twisted-pair cabling, part of the IEEE 802.3u specification."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-0d86bcb46f981cf5384a0d47ea9a60d4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;GIGABIT ETHERNET&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"TECHNOLOGY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Gigabit Ethernet is a version of Ethernet that supports data rates of 1 Gbps, standardized under IEEE 802.3z and 802.3ab."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-0d86bcb46f981cf5384a0d47ea9a60d4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;10GBE&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"TECHNOLOGY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"10 Gigabit Ethernet (10GbE) is a high-speed Ethernet standard providing data rates of 10 Gbps, used in backbone networks and data centers."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-0d86bcb46f981cf5384a0d47ea9a60d4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;IP&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"IP (Internet Protocol) is mentioned in the context of network layer functionality and the concepts 'everything over IP' and 'IP over everything', indicating its foundational role in internet communication."&lt;SEP&gt;"IP (Internet Protocol) is the principal network-layer protocol in the TCP/IP suite responsible for addressing and routing packets across interconnected networks; standardized by the IETF."&lt;SEP&gt;"Internet Protocol (IP) is the principal communications protocol in the Internet protocol suite for relaying datagrams across network boundaries."&lt;SEP&gt;"Internet Protocol is the principal communications protocol in the network layer for relaying datagrams across network boundaries."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d8da4662c1f51095b9efcbe2f2cbe556&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b44f82ed51f5dd17e63648288cc859c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d86bcb46f981cf5384a0d47ea9a60d4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ffae866e92afc591d3a1c26480aeeb3</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 8}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 38}, {"level": 2, "cluster": 57}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;ARP&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"TECHNOLOGY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Address Resolution Protocol (ARP) is a protocol used to map IP addresses to physical MAC addresses on a local network."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-0d86bcb46f981cf5384a0d47ea9a60d4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;ICMP&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"TECHNOLOGY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Internet Control Message Protocol (ICMP) is a supporting protocol in the Internet protocol suite used for error reporting and diagnostic functions, such as ping and traceroute."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-0d86bcb46f981cf5384a0d47ea9a60d4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;IPV6&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"TECHNOLOGY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Internet Protocol version 6 (IPv6) is the most recent version of the Internet Protocol, designed to replace IPv4 and provide a vastly larger address space."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-0d86bcb46f981cf5384a0d47ea9a60d4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;RIP&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"TECHNOLOGY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Routing Information Protocol (RIP) is an interior gateway protocol that uses hop count as a routing metric, primarily used in small to medium-sized networks."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-0d86bcb46f981cf5384a0d47ea9a60d4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;OSPF&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"TECHNOLOGY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Open Shortest Path First (OSPF) is a link-state routing protocol for IP networks, known for its fast convergence and scalability within autonomous systems."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-0d86bcb46f981cf5384a0d47ea9a60d4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;BGP&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"TECHNOLOGY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Border Gateway Protocol (BGP) is the exterior gateway protocol used to exchange routing and reachability information among autonomous systems on the Internet."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-0d86bcb46f981cf5384a0d47ea9a60d4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;IGMP&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"TECHNOLOGY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Internet Group Management Protocol (IGMP) is used by IPv4 hosts and adjacent routers to establish multicast group memberships."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-0d86bcb46f981cf5384a0d47ea9a60d4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;VPN&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"TECHNOLOGY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Virtual Private Network (VPN) is a secure tunneling technology that allows private network communications over public networks like the Internet."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-0d86bcb46f981cf5384a0d47ea9a60d4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;NAT&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"TECHNOLOGY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Network Address Translation (NAT) is a method of remapping IP address spaces by modifying network address information in packet headers while in transit."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-0d86bcb46f981cf5384a0d47ea9a60d4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;MPLS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"TECHNOLOGY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Multiprotocol Label Switching (MPLS) is a protocol designed to speed up and shape traffic flows across enterprise and service provider networks using label switching."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-0d86bcb46f981cf5384a0d47ea9a60d4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;SDN&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"TECHNOLOGY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Software-Defined Networking (SDN) is a network architecture approach that separates the control plane from the data plane, enabling centralized, programmable network management."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-0d86bcb46f981cf5384a0d47ea9a60d4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;UDP&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"UDP (User Datagram Protocol) is a core transport layer protocol used for lightweight, connectionless communication across networks."&lt;SEP&gt;"UDP (User Datagram Protocol) is a transport-layer protocol in the TCP/IP model that enables fast, connectionless communication with no delivery guarantees; maintained as an IETF standard."&lt;SEP&gt;"User Datagram Protocol (UDP) is a lightweight transport layer protocol that provides minimal error checking and no guaranteed delivery, often used for real-time applications."&lt;SEP&gt;"User Datagram Protocol is a transport layer protocol that enables fast but unreliable data transmission, often used for time-sensitive applications."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d8da4662c1f51095b9efcbe2f2cbe556&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d86bcb46f981cf5384a0d47ea9a60d4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ffae866e92afc591d3a1c26480aeeb3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-39b5624a41742080541fba0136f5dc34</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 8}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 38}, {"level": 2, "cluster": 56}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;TCP&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Although TCP (Transmission Control Protocol) is technically a protocol, within the context of this text it is treated as an internationally recognized technical acronym used in networking discourse and referenced as a standard term in both English and Chinese technical literature."&lt;SEP&gt;"TCP (Transmission Control Protocol) is a core transport-layer protocol in the TCP/IP suite that provides reliable, connection-oriented communication between processes on networked hosts; standardized by the IETF."&lt;SEP&gt;"TCP (Transmission Control Protocol) is a fundamental transport layer protocol known for reliable, connection-oriented data transmission over the internet."&lt;SEP&gt;"TCP (Transmission Control Protocol) is referenced as a core protocol in computer networking, used for reliable data transmission and connection establishment."&lt;SEP&gt;"Transmission Control Protocol is a core transport layer protocol that provides reliable, ordered, and error-checked delivery of data between applications."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d8da4662c1f51095b9efcbe2f2cbe556&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4b9e864a515f0d66754933635238c92d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b44f82ed51f5dd17e63648288cc859c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ffae866e92afc591d3a1c26480aeeb3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-39b5624a41742080541fba0136f5dc34</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 8}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 38}, {"level": 2, "cluster": 56}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;DNS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"DNS (Domain Name System) is a distributed naming system and protocol used on the internet to translate human-readable domain names into IP addresses; it functions as a critical infrastructure service coordinated by various technical bodies including ICANN."&lt;SEP&gt;"DNS (Domain Name System) is an application-layer system that translates human-readable domain names into IP addresses for network communication."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-4ffae866e92afc591d3a1c26480aeeb3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-39b5624a41742080541fba0136f5dc34</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 8}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 38}, {"level": 2, "cluster": 55}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;FTP&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"FTP (File Transfer Protocol) is a standard network protocol used for transferring files between a client and server on a computer network."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-39b5624a41742080541fba0136f5dc34</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;TFTP&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"TFTP (Trivial File Transfer Protocol) is a simplified version of FTP used for basic file transfers, typically in local networks."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-39b5624a41742080541fba0136f5dc34</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;TELNET&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"TELNET is a network protocol used for remote terminal access to devices over a network."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-39b5624a41742080541fba0136f5dc34</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;WWW&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"WWW (World Wide Web) is an information system that enables content sharing over the internet via hyperlinked documents and resources."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-39b5624a41742080541fba0136f5dc34</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;HTTP&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"HTTP (Hypertext Transfer Protocol) is an application-layer protocol used for distributed, collaborative information systems like the World Wide Web."&lt;SEP&gt;"HTTP (Hypertext Transfer Protocol) is an application-layer protocol used for distributed, collaborative, hypermedia information systems, primarily on the World Wide Web; it is standardized by the Internet Engineering Task Force (IETF) and the World Wide Web Consortium (W3C)."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-4ffae866e92afc591d3a1c26480aeeb3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-39b5624a41742080541fba0136f5dc34</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 8}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 38}, {"level": 2, "cluster": 55}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;SMTP&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"SMTP (Simple Mail Transfer Protocol) is a protocol for sending email messages between servers and from clients to servers."&lt;SEP&gt;"SMTP (Simple Mail Transfer Protocol) is a standard communication protocol for sending email across the internet, maintained and standardized by the IETF."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-4ffae866e92afc591d3a1c26480aeeb3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-39b5624a41742080541fba0136f5dc34</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 8}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 38}, {"level": 2, "cluster": 55}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;POP3&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"POP3 (Post Office Protocol version 3) is an email retrieval protocol that downloads messages from a server to a local client."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-39b5624a41742080541fba0136f5dc34</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;IMAP&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"IMAP (Internet Message Access Protocol) is an email protocol that allows users to manage messages directly on a mail server."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-39b5624a41742080541fba0136f5dc34</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;DHCP&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"DHCP (Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol) is a network management protocol used to automatically assign IP addresses and other configuration parameters to devices."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-39b5624a41742080541fba0136f5dc34</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;SNMP&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"SNMP (Simple Network Management Protocol) is a protocol for managing and monitoring network devices and their functions."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-39b5624a41742080541fba0136f5dc34</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;P2P&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"P2P (Peer-to-Peer) refers to decentralized networking architectures where peers share resources directly without centralized servers."&lt;SEP&gt;"Peer-to-Peer (P2P) is a decentralized communication model where each party has equivalent capabilities and can initiate communication sessions, contrasting with client-server models."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d8da4662c1f51095b9efcbe2f2cbe556&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-39b5624a41742080541fba0136f5dc34</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;AQM&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"AQM (Active Queue Management) is a technique used in network routers to manage congestion by proactively dropping or marking packets."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-39b5624a41742080541fba0136f5dc34</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;SACK&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"SACK (Selective Acknowledgment) is a TCP feature that improves performance by allowing receivers to acknowledge non-contiguous blocks of received data."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-39b5624a41742080541fba0136f5dc34</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;MIME&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"MIME (Multipurpose Internet Mail Extensions) is an extension to email protocols that supports text in character sets other than ASCII and attachments of various media types."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-39b5624a41742080541fba0136f5dc34</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;SMI&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"SMI (Structure of Management Information) is a standard used with SNMP to define rules for describing managed objects in a MIB."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-39b5624a41742080541fba0136f5dc34</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;MIB&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"MIB (Management Information Base) is a database used in SNMP to store manageable network object definitions."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-39b5624a41742080541fba0136f5dc34</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;计算机网络&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Computer networks (计算机网络) are described as structured systems enabling information exchange, forming the backbone of the information age and supporting digital, networked, and informational societal functions."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-41cb6db802a71038c261f08ac9a7f0ca</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;802.11 局域网&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The 802.11 LAN (局域网) refers to a standardized wireless local area network technology defined by IEEE, covering physical and MAC layer protocols for WLAN communication."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-41cb6db802a71038c261f08ac9a7f0ca</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;LTE 网络&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"LTE Network (LTE 网络) is a cellular mobile communication system discussed in the context of its integration with the internet, representing a key evolution in mobile broadband infrastructure."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-41cb6db802a71038c261f08ac9a7f0ca</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;移动 IP&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Mobile IP (移动 IP) is an event-like technical framework introduced to support mobility in IP networks, allowing devices to maintain connectivity while changing network points of attachment."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-41cb6db802a71038c261f08ac9a7f0ca</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;防火墙&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Firewalls (防火墙) are security systems described as part of system security infrastructure, designed to monitor and control incoming and outgoing network traffic based on predetermined rules."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-41cb6db802a71038c261f08ac9a7f0ca</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;入侵检测系统&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Intrusion Detection Systems (入侵检测系统) are security mechanisms integrated into network defense strategies to identify malicious activities or policy violations."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-41cb6db802a71038c261f08ac9a7f0ca</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;RTP&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Real-time Transport Protocol (RTP, 实时运输协议) is a standardized protocol used for delivering audio and video over IP networks, particularly in interactive multimedia applications."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-41cb6db802a71038c261f08ac9a7f0ca</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;RTCP&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Real-time Transport Control Protocol (RTCP, 实时运输控制协议) works alongside RTP to provide out-of-band statistics and control information for an RTP session."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-41cb6db802a71038c261f08ac9a7f0ca</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;SIP&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Session Initiation Protocol (SIP, 会话发起协议) is an application-layer signaling protocol used to establish, modify, and terminate multimedia sessions such as voice and video calls over IP networks."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-41cb6db802a71038c261f08ac9a7f0ca</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;H.323&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"H.323 is a suite of ITU-T recommendations for multimedia communication over packet-switched networks, referenced in the context of IP telephony protocols."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-41cb6db802a71038c261f08ac9a7f0ca</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;RTSP&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Real-Time Streaming Protocol (RTSP, 实时流式协议) is an application-layer protocol used to control streaming media servers, enabling functions like play, pause, and stop for media delivery."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-41cb6db802a71038c261f08ac9a7f0ca</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;INTSERV&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Integrated Services (综合服务 IntServ) is a framework for providing Quality of Service (QoS) on IP networks through resource reservation using RSVP."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-41cb6db802a71038c261f08ac9a7f0ca</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;DIFFSERV&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Differentiated Services (区分服务 DiffServ) is a QoS architecture that classifies and manages network traffic to provide different levels of service for various types of data flows."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-41cb6db802a71038c261f08ac9a7f0ca</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;RSVP&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Resource Reservation Protocol (资源预留协议 RSVP) is a signaling protocol used in IntServ to reserve resources across a network for quality-of-service guarantees."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-41cb6db802a71038c261f08ac9a7f0ca</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;对称密钥密码体制&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Symmetric-key cryptography (对称密钥密码体制) is a cryptographic system where the same key is used for both encryption and decryption, discussed as a foundational security method."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-41cb6db802a71038c261f08ac9a7f0ca</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;公钥密码体制&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Public-key cryptography (公钥密码体制) is a cryptographic approach using key pairs (public and private), enabling secure communication without prior key exchange."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-41cb6db802a71038c261f08ac9a7f0ca</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;报文鉴别&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Message authentication (报文鉴别) is a security process ensuring the integrity and origin of transmitted messages."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-41cb6db802a71038c261f08ac9a7f0ca</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;实体鉴别&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Entity authentication (实体鉴别) is a process verifying the identity of a communicating party in a network interaction."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-41cb6db802a71038c261f08ac9a7f0ca</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;全国科学技术名词审定委员会&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The National Committee for the Review and Standardization of Scientific and Technical Terminology is a Chinese organization that recommended '因特网' as the official Chinese translation for 'Internet'."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-4b9e864a515f0d66754933635238c92d</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 5}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 25}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;中国&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"GEO"</data>
  <data key="d1">"China is implicitly referenced as the geographic and linguistic context where terms like '互联网', '因特网', and usage patterns such as '网民', '网银', and '网购' are prevalent in media, government documents, and daily life."&lt;SEP&gt;"China is the country where the described developments in computer networking and internet infrastructure took place, including the establishment of national backbones and key internet milestones."&lt;SEP&gt;"China is the geographic and national context where all the described internet companies were founded and operate, and where these technological developments significantly impacted internet adoption."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-4b9e864a515f0d66754933635238c92d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-779592fbef47ea27eab563c9f2b5fb8a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3cf7f9315ae398e3c73bd500746d26d8</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 11}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 50}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;三网融合&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"'Three-Network Convergence' (三网融合) refers to the theoretical integration of telecommunications networks, cable television networks, and computer networks into a single unified network capable of delivering all associated services. This concept has faced practical challenges due to economic interests and administrative jurisdiction issues."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-4b9e864a515f0d66754933635238c92d</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 5}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 27}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;20世纪90年代以后互联网的飞速发展&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"This event describes the rapid global expansion of the Internet from the 1990s onward, transforming it from a U.S.-centric academic network into the world’s dominant commercial and communication infrastructure."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-4b9e864a515f0d66754933635238c92d</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 5}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 25}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;TELECOMMUNICATIONS NETWORK&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The global telecommunications network is formed by interconnected telephone exchanges and supports worldwide voice communication using circuit switching."&lt;SEP&gt;"The telecommunications network is one of the three major traditional networks, originally providing services such as telephone, telegraph, and fax. It has increasingly incorporated computer networking technologies and expanded its service scope over time."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-4b9e864a515f0d66754933635238c92d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca0c22897ac4785fecc70607badaf9cb</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 5}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 27}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;CABLE TELEVISION NETWORK&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The cable television network is a major traditional network that delivers television programming to users. Like telecommunications networks, it has begun integrating modern computer network technologies to broaden its capabilities."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-4b9e864a515f0d66754933635238c92d</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 5}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 27}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;COMPUTER NETWORK&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A computer network enables data file transfer between computers and has become the fastest-growing and most central network type in the information age, forming the foundation of modern digital society and evolving to support voice, video, and multimedia services."&lt;SEP&gt;"A computer network is defined as a system of interconnected nodes (such as computers, hubs, switches, or routers) linked by communication channels. It serves as the foundational structure for data transmission and forms the building block of larger internetworks."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-4b9e864a515f0d66754933635238c92d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd06b8e7be2529cda8d9408c8ba624d5</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 5}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 30}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;INFORMATION AGE&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The Information Age is a historical period characterized by digitization, networking, and informatization, with computer networks at its core. It represents a societal transformation driven by rapid information exchange and network infrastructure."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-4b9e864a515f0d66754933635238c92d</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 5}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 30}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;GLOBAL COMMERCIAL INTERNET&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The evolution of the Internet from a U.S.-based academic and research network into a worldwide commercial (paid) network marks a pivotal event in global communications and economic development."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-4b9e864a515f0d66754933635238c92d</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;ONLINE BANKING&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Online banking (网银) is a significant application of the Internet that allows users to conduct financial transactions remotely, reducing the need for physical bank visits and transforming traditional banking practices."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-4b9e864a515f0d66754933635238c92d</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 5}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 25}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;E-COMMERCE&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"E-commerce (网购 or online shopping) is a major Internet-driven event that has reshaped consumer behavior by enabling convenient, cost-effective purchasing of goods and services over the Internet, replacing or supplementing physical retail."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-4b9e864a515f0d66754933635238c92d</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 5}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 25}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;SOCIAL COMMUNICATION VIA INTERNET&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The widespread use of the Internet for social interaction—through email, messaging apps, and video sharing—constitutes a transformative societal event that has diminished traditional postal communication and redefined interpersonal connectivity."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-4b9e864a515f0d66754933635238c92d</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 5}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 25}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;美国&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"GEO"</data>
  <data key="d1">"美国 (the United States) is identified as the origin country of the internet, marking its historical and geographical roots."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-561c228fa7944971c082eed5e584dc98</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 9}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 39}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;互联网瘫痪事件&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"互联网瘫痪事件 refers to a hypothetical scenario where internet services in a city suddenly fail, leading to widespread disruption in communication, finance, transportation, commerce, and public services, illustrating society's deep dependence on the internet."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-561c228fa7944971c082eed5e584dc98</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 9}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 39}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;电子邮件&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"电子邮件 (email) is an internet application that enables users to send and receive messages electronically; it has significantly disrupted traditional communication methods like telegrams and is a key example of internet-based information sharing."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-561c228fa7944971c082eed5e584dc98</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;网络电话&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"网络电话 (Voice over IP or internet telephony) is an internet-based communication service that allows voice calls over the internet, leading to a sharp decline in traditional long-distance and international phone usage."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-561c228fa7944971c082eed5e584dc98</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;网上购物&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Online shopping is highlighted as a rapidly growing internet-based economic activity in China, reflecting the integration of e-commerce into daily life."&lt;SEP&gt;"网上购物 (online shopping) refers to the practice of purchasing goods via the internet, which has caused many physical retail stores to close due to its convenience and efficiency."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-561c228fa7944971c082eed5e584dc98&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-779592fbef47ea27eab563c9f2b5fb8a</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;网约车&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"网约车 (ride-hailing services via internet platforms) is an internet-enabled transportation service that has significantly disrupted the traditional taxi industry."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-561c228fa7944971c082eed5e584dc98</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;网上支付&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Online payment is noted as a swiftly adopted internet service in China, indicating the digitization of financial transactions among netizens."&lt;SEP&gt;"网上支付 (online payment systems) enables digital financial transactions over the internet, drastically reducing foot traffic in bank branches and transforming how people manage finances."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-561c228fa7944971c082eed5e584dc98&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-779592fbef47ea27eab563c9f2b5fb8a</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;计算机病毒传播&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"计算机病毒传播 (spread of computer viruses) is a harmful activity conducted over the internet, disrupting normal data transmission and posing cybersecurity risks."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-561c228fa7944971c082eed5e584dc98</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;网络犯罪&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"网络犯罪 encompasses illegal activities such as stealing state secrets, bank fraud, online scams, spreading rumors, and distributing harmful content via the internet."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-561c228fa7944971c082eed5e584dc98</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;青少年网络游戏沉迷&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"青少年网络游戏沉迷 (youth addiction to online gaming) describes a social issue where teenagers abandon their studies to spend excessive time in internet cafes playing games."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-561c228fa7944971c082eed5e584dc98</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;互联网+&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"互联网+ ('Internet Plus') is a strategic concept representing the integration of internet technologies with traditional industries to foster new economic ecosystems and enhance productivity across sectors."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-561c228fa7944971c082eed5e584dc98</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;银行&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Chinese banking institutions built dedicated wide-area computer networks in the late 1980s to support secure and rapid data transmission."&lt;SEP&gt;"银行 (banks) are financial institutions whose operations—such as deposits, withdrawals, and bill payments—are heavily reliant on internet connectivity; many physical branches have seen reduced activity due to the rise of online banking."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-561c228fa7944971c082eed5e584dc98&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-779592fbef47ea27eab563c9f2b5fb8a</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;实体商店&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"实体商店 (brick-and-mortar stores) are traditional retail businesses that have suffered closures due to competition from convenient and efficient online shopping platforms."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-561c228fa7944971c082eed5e584dc98</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;出租车行业&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"出租车行业 (the taxi industry) is a transportation sector that has experienced major disruption and competitive pressure from internet-based ride-hailing services."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-561c228fa7944971c082eed5e584dc98</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;图书馆&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"图书馆 (libraries) are institutions that now depend on internet-connected systems for catalog searches and access to digital resources; during an internet outage, users cannot retrieve needed books or materials."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-561c228fa7944971c082eed5e584dc98</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;股市&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"股市 (stock market) refers to financial exchanges whose trading systems rely on internet infrastructure; internet failure would halt trading activities entirely."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-561c228fa7944971c082eed5e584dc98</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;售票处&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"售票处 (ticket offices for flights and trains) are service points that require real-time internet access to check seat availability and process sales; they become nonfunctional during internet outages."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-561c228fa7944971c082eed5e584dc98</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;网吧&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"网吧 (internet cafes) are commercial venues where individuals, particularly youth, access the internet for gaming and other activities; they are implicated in cases of gaming addiction among teenagers."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-561c228fa7944971c082eed5e584dc98</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;ROUTER&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A router is a networking device that forwards data packets between computer networks, operating in the core part of the Internet using store-and-forward packet switching."&lt;SEP&gt;"A router is described as a specialized computer in the network core that performs packet switching and forwards data packets; it plays a critical role in Internet infrastructure."&lt;SEP&gt;"A router is treated as an active network entity that participates in forwarding packets based on routing tables; in this context, it functions as an organizational node within the Internet's architecture."&lt;SEP&gt;"In the context of networking, a router is a specialized computing device that forwards data packets across interconnected networks. It operates in the core part of the internet, using routing tables and packet headers to determine optimal transmission paths."&lt;SEP&gt;"Routers are described as indispensable components of the internet, functioning as specialized computers with CPUs, memory, and operating systems. They enable interconnection between networks and are distinct from hosts."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-ca0c22897ac4785fecc70607badaf9cb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8da4662c1f51095b9efcbe2f2cbe556&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b07dbbacc9a0b17e8854aaf7b97228e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd06b8e7be2529cda8d9408c8ba624d5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c827098a559a559af3c8df103949b45</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 5}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 25}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;HOST&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A host refers to an end system (such as a computer or server) that runs application processes and participates in network communication by sending and receiving data through the layered protocol stack."&lt;SEP&gt;"A host refers to an end-user computer or device located at the edge of the internet that processes information for users and exchanges data with other hosts via the network."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hosts refer to computers connected to a network that are used for communication and data exchange. The text specifies that hosts are conventionally drawn outside the cloud symbol when discussing communication between computers."&lt;SEP&gt;"In networking context, a host refers to any computer or device connected to a network that can provide information resources, services, or applications to users or other hosts on the network; specifically mentioned as operating in the edge part of the Internet."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d8da4662c1f51095b9efcbe2f2cbe556&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b07dbbacc9a0b17e8854aaf7b97228e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c827098a559a559af3c8df103949b45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd06b8e7be2529cda8d9408c8ba624d5</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 5}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 29}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;HUB&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A hub is a networking device that serves as a common connection point for nodes in a computer network, mentioned as one of the possible types of nodes in the network."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-bd06b8e7be2529cda8d9408c8ba624d5</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 5}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 30}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;SWITCH&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A switch is a networking device that connects devices within a computer network and uses packet switching to forward data to the correct destination; it is listed among the node types in the described network architecture."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-bd06b8e7be2529cda8d9408c8ba624d5</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 5}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 30}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;ARPANET&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"ARPANET is an early computer network that implemented packet switching technology and demonstrated low end-to-end latency across the United States during its initial operation."&lt;SEP&gt;"ARPANET was a pioneering U.S. computer network and the predecessor to the modern Internet, playing a foundational role in the development of networked communication."&lt;SEP&gt;"ARPANET was an early computer network whose design introduced the concept of layering to manage complexity in network communication, forming a foundational influence on later network architectures."&lt;SEP&gt;"ARPANET was an early computer network whose development experience demonstrated that complex network protocols should adopt a hierarchical structure."&lt;SEP&gt;"ARPANET was an early wide-area packet-switched network developed in the late 20th century, demonstrating sub-0.1-second end-to-end latency across the U.S. under normal load."&lt;SEP&gt;"ARPANET was the first packet-switching network created by the U.S. Department of Defense in 1969. It initially functioned as a single network but later evolved into an interconnected network, forming the precursor to the modern Internet. It officially shut down in 1990 after completing its experimental mission."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-076a966631561cc724a628cc319a15f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8da4662c1f51095b9efcbe2f2cbe556&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a1b5e11d873f879fd9610fda65292066&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8fe573a1edca72839801eaf0704a5d62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dbda231b683f35c9fc91e9cc64fa2b9</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 0}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 12}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;ARPA&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"ARPA (Advanced Research Projects Agency) is the U.S. government agency that initiated research into interconnecting multiple networks, including packet radio networks, leading to the development of the early internet architecture."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-076a966631561cc724a628cc319a15f3</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 0}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 12}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;NSF&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"NSF (National Science Foundation) is a U.S. government agency that, starting in 1985, built the NSFNET—a three-tiered computer network consisting of backbone, regional, and campus/enterprise networks—becoming a major component of the Internet."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-076a966631561cc724a628cc319a15f3</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 0}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 15}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;NSFNET&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"NSFNET was a U.S. government-funded network that served as an early internet backbone until it was replaced by commercial internet backbones starting in 1993."&lt;SEP&gt;"NSFNET was a three-level computer network developed by the National Science Foundation, comprising a backbone network, regional networks, and campus or enterprise networks, covering major U.S. universities and research institutes and forming a core part of the Internet during the late 1980s and early 1990s."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7cb534a20cec43f62bad499beb758a78&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-076a966631561cc724a628cc319a15f3</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 0}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 15}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;UNITED STATES&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"GEO"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The United States is referenced as the geographic region where ARPANET operated, specifically noting communication between the East and West Coasts."&lt;SEP&gt;"The United States is referenced as the home country of IBM, which announced SNA in 1974."&lt;SEP&gt;"The United States is referenced as the origin country of ARPANET, which later evolved into the Internet."&lt;SEP&gt;"The United States is the country where key developments in internet infrastructure occurred, including the creation of ARPANET by the Department of Defense and the deployment of NSFNET by the National Science Foundation."&lt;SEP&gt;"The United States serves as the geographic context for ARPANET’s early deployment, specifically referenced in terms of east-west coast data transmission."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-8fe573a1edca72839801eaf0704a5d62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8da4662c1f51095b9efcbe2f2cbe556&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dbda231b683f35c9fc91e9cc64fa2b9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-076a966631561cc724a628cc319a15f3</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 0}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 12}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;TCP/IP&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"TCP/IP is a suite of communication protocols used to interconnect network devices on the Internet. It became the standard protocol on ARPANET in 1983, enabling all compatible computers to communicate over the emerging Internet."&lt;SEP&gt;"TCP/IP is the suite of communication protocols used by the Internet to enable data exchange across interconnected networks; it includes the Transmission Control Protocol (TCP) and Internet Protocol (IP)."&lt;SEP&gt;"TCP/IP refers to the Transmission Control Protocol/Internet Protocol suite, described as a four-layer communication architecture widely used in the internet, though criticized for lacking clear distinctions between service, interface, and protocol."&lt;SEP&gt;"TCP/IP refers to the suite of communication protocols that became the de facto global standard for internet communication, gaining widespread adoption in the late 20th century ahead of the OSI model and dominating the market despite not being an official international standard initially."&lt;SEP&gt;"TCP/IP refers to the suite of communication protocols used to interconnect network devices on the internet, described here as a four-layer architecture widely implemented in practice."&lt;SEP&gt;"TCP/IP refers to the suite of communication protocols used to interconnect network devices on the internet, often associated with foundational internet standards organizations like the IETF, though commonly treated as a conceptual framework rather than a formal organization; in this context, it represents the protocol architecture endorsed by internet engineering communities."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-e05b6bd70dc7a04d2758501d65696eff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-076a966631561cc724a628cc319a15f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8da4662c1f51095b9efcbe2f2cbe556&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77d64847854ffc2a5d57b6da6e7bc7d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ffae866e92afc591d3a1c26480aeeb3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d24b4f467e6a4552877bf9e42c614b2</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 8}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 38}, {"level": 2, "cluster": 55}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;U.S. DEPARTMENT OF DEFENSE&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The U.S. Department of Defense created ARPANET in 1969 as the first packet-switching network, laying the foundation for the development of the Internet."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-076a966631561cc724a628cc319a15f3</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;1983&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The year 1983 marks the official birth of the Internet, when TCP/IP was adopted as the standard protocol on ARPANET, enabling universal communication among diverse networks."&lt;SEP&gt;"The year when the formal document for the OSI basic reference model was established as ISO 7498, marking a key milestone in networking standardization efforts."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7d24b4f467e6a4552877bf9e42c614b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-076a966631561cc724a628cc319a15f3</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;1990&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The year 1990 is when ARPANET was formally decommissioned after fulfilling its experimental role in the development of network interconnection technologies."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-076a966631561cc724a628cc319a15f3</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;1992&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"In 1992, the number of hosts on the Internet exceeded one million, signaling rapid global adoption beyond academic and government use."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-076a966631561cc724a628cc319a15f3</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;1993&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"In 1993, the Internet backbone speed was upgraded to 45 Mbit/s (T3 rate), reflecting growing demand and infrastructure scaling."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-076a966631561cc724a628cc319a15f3</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;NATIONAL SCIENCE FOUNDATION&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The National Science Foundation (NSF) is a U.S. federal agency that funded and oversaw the creation of NSFNET, a critical infrastructure that expanded Internet access to universities and research institutions across the United States."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-076a966631561cc724a628cc319a15f3</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;U.S. GOVERNMENT&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The U.S. Government funded NSFNET and was responsible for early internet operations before transitioning to a commercial ISP model."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7cb534a20cec43f62bad499beb758a78</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 0}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 15}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;ISP&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"An ISP (Internet Service Provider) is an organization that provides internet connectivity services to end systems and may also own its own host systems (end systems)."&lt;SEP&gt;"ISP (Internet Service Provider) is an organization that provides internet connectivity to users, guaranteeing a certain data transmission rate only between the user's premises and the ISP's router."&lt;SEP&gt;"ISP (Internet Service Provider) is the organization that provides Internet access to end users, mentioned in the context of paid broadband service."&lt;SEP&gt;"ISP (Internet Service Provider) refers to commercial or institutional entities that provide internet access, IP addresses, and networking infrastructure to users; ISPs operate at multiple levels: backbone, regional, and local."&lt;SEP&gt;"ISP (Internet Service Provider) refers to organizations that provide internet connectivity services; the text notes that large backbone ISPs often cap channel utilization at 50% to manage network delay."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-0174bb3213f0a9872650fbd9b001dae3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-febb982eeee1d6798571d55df8b4d130&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7cb534a20cec43f62bad499beb758a78&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2ab20554e89073ba513e052dbf5667c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30cc6309bbf7dc040e88d043d89a8722</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 4}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 22}, {"level": 2, "cluster": 53}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;CHINA TELECOM&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"China Telecom is one of China's most prominent Internet Service Providers (ISPs), offering internet connectivity and related services."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7cb534a20cec43f62bad499beb758a78</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 4}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 22}, {"level": 2, "cluster": 53}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;CHINA UNICOM&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"China Unicom is a major Chinese Internet Service Provider (ISP), providing telecommunications and internet services nationwide."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7cb534a20cec43f62bad499beb758a78</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 4}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 22}, {"level": 2, "cluster": 53}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;CHINA MOBILE&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"China Mobile is a leading Chinese Internet Service Provider (ISP), known for mobile and broadband internet services."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7cb534a20cec43f62bad499beb758a78</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 4}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 22}, {"level": 2, "cluster": 53}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;IXP&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"An Internet Exchange Point (IXP) is an organization that facilitates the interconnection of multiple ISPs via network switches, enabling direct, high-speed, and often cost-free peering of data packets between regional ISPs to improve traffic distribution, reduce latency, and lower costs."&lt;SEP&gt;"IXP (Internet eXchange Point) is an infrastructure that enables direct interconnection and packet exchange between networks, improving traffic efficiency and reducing reliance on third-party transit providers."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7cb534a20cec43f62bad499beb758a78&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c4a2f7654873f1e1c6f37434762f36ae</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 4}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 21}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;HOST A&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"GEO"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Host A is a computer system or machine located in a networked environment that runs the client program in the client-server communication model, as illustrated in Figure 1-5."&lt;SEP&gt;"Host A is a networked computer device located at the edge of the internet, functioning as a client in the client-server communication model. It runs an application-layer client process that initiates requests to server processes on other hosts."&lt;SEP&gt;"Host A is a representative end-user device or system communicating over the internet through multiple layers of ISPs; used illustratively in the text to demonstrate data flow."&lt;SEP&gt;"Host A represents an endpoint device connected to the Internet with a 100 Mbit/s link, used as an example in throughput discussion."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7cb534a20cec43f62bad499beb758a78&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2ab20554e89073ba513e052dbf5667c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7076395e2a9a9ea6cdf6d289a0503201&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ad6543c9c55fc16571200afb61505ac0</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 4}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 22}, {"level": 2, "cluster": 54}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;HOST B&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"GEO"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Host B is a computer system or machine that runs the server program in the client-server model, providing services to Host A upon request, as shown in Figure 1-5."&lt;SEP&gt;"Host B is a networked computer device situated at the edge of the internet, acting as a client in client-server interactions. It contains a client process that communicates with server processes on other hosts, such as Host C."&lt;SEP&gt;"Host B is another representative end-user device or system that communicates with Host A across the layered ISP structure; used as a counterpart in the communication example."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7cb534a20cec43f62bad499beb758a78&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ad6543c9c55fc16571200afb61505ac0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7076395e2a9a9ea6cdf6d289a0503201</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 4}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 22}, {"level": 2, "cluster": 54}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;GLOBAL INTERNET&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The Global Internet refers to the worldwide, multi-tiered network infrastructure formed by countless interconnected ISPs, evolving from government-managed systems to a decentralized commercial ecosystem."&lt;SEP&gt;"The worldwide computer network based on TCP/IP protocols, described as the largest and most pervasive network covering the globe, which did not adopt the OSI standards."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7cb534a20cec43f62bad499beb758a78&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d24b4f467e6a4552877bf9e42c614b2</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 4}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 23}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;TRANSITION FROM NSFNET TO COMMERCIAL BACKBONES&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"This event marks the shift beginning in 1993 when the U.S. government ceased operating the internet backbone, allowing commercial ISPs to take over internet infrastructure."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7cb534a20cec43f62bad499beb758a78</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 0}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 15}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;BACKBONE ISP&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Backbone ISPs are large-scale Internet Service Providers that operate high-speed national or global networks (e.g., 10 Gbit/s or higher), forming the top tier of the internet’s hierarchical ISP structure and interconnecting regional ISPs."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7cb534a20cec43f62bad499beb758a78</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 4}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 23}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;REGIONAL ISP&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Regional ISPs are Internet Service Providers that operate across a broader area than local ISPs, interconnect via IXPs for peering, and avoid upstream transit costs for mutual traffic by exchanging packets directly at high speed."&lt;SEP&gt;"Regional ISPs are mid-tier Internet Service Providers that serve a specific geographic region, connecting local ISPs to backbone ISPs and operating at lower data rates than backbone providers."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7cb534a20cec43f62bad499beb758a78&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c4a2f7654873f1e1c6f37434762f36ae</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 4}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 23}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;LOCAL ISP&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Local ISPs are Internet Service Providers that serve end-users in a limited geographic area and may connect to regional ISPs or IXPs; they typically pay fees when forwarding packets through IXPs to higher-tier networks."&lt;SEP&gt;"Local ISPs are the lowest-tier Internet Service Providers that offer direct internet access to end users, including individuals, businesses, universities, or non-profit organizations; they may connect to regional or backbone ISPs."&lt;SEP&gt;"Local Internet Service Providers (ISPs) own and manage access networks that connect end-user systems to the internet via edge routers, using various broadband access technologies."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7cb534a20cec43f62bad499beb758a78&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-982f03b0a500a9e6bd7272aaa9e050d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c4a2f7654873f1e1c6f37434762f36ae</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 4}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 23}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;IP ADDRESS MANAGEMENT AUTHORITY&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The IP address management authority is the entity responsible for allocating large blocks of IP addresses exclusively to qualified ISPs rather than individual users, ensuring structured and scalable internet addressing."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7cb534a20cec43f62bad499beb758a78</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 4}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 22}, {"level": 2, "cluster": 53}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;UNIVERSITY&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Universities are mentioned as examples of non-profit institutions that can operate as local ISPs by running their own networks and providing internet services to their communities."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7cb534a20cec43f62bad499beb758a78</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 4}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 23}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;ENTERPRISE&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Enterprises (businesses) are cited as potential local ISPs when they maintain internal networks and provide internet connectivity to their employees."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterprises are cited as entities that can own end systems on the Internet, participating in communication and resource sharing as part of the edge."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7cb534a20cec43f62bad499beb758a78&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30cc6309bbf7dc040e88d043d89a8722</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 4}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 23}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;INTERNET EXCHANGE POINT (IXP)&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"GEO"</data>
  <data key="d1">"An Internet Exchange Point (IXP) is a physical infrastructure location where multiple networks interconnect to exchange internet traffic directly, often situated in major data centers or metropolitan hubs."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7cb534a20cec43f62bad499beb758a78</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 4}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 23}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;FIGURE 1-3&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Figure 1-3 represents a conceptual illustration of the three-layer ISP hierarchy of the internet, showing how Host A communicates with Host B through various ISP levels and an IXP."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7cb534a20cec43f62bad499beb758a78</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 4}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 22}, {"level": 2, "cluster": 54}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;DE-CIX&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"DE-CIX is a major Internet Exchange Point located in Frankfurt, Germany, serving as a key internet hub in Europe with a peak throughput of 7.72 Tbit/s as of August 2020."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-c4a2f7654873f1e1c6f37434762f36ae</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;W-PCH&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"W-PCH is an organization that published statistics in August 2020 reporting the global count of IXPs, including regional distributions such as those in China."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-c4a2f7654873f1e1c6f37434762f36ae</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 4}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 21}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;CERN&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"CERN (European Organization for Nuclear Research) is the organization that developed the World Wide Web (WWW), which became a major driver of the internet's exponential growth starting in the 1990s by making the internet accessible to non-specialists."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-c4a2f7654873f1e1c6f37434762f36ae</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;INTERNET SOCIETY (ISOC)&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The Internet Society (ISOC) is an international organization founded in 1992 to manage and promote the development and use of the internet globally after it was no longer under U.S. government control."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-c4a2f7654873f1e1c6f37434762f36ae</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 7}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 35}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;INTERNET ARCHITECTURE BOARD (IAB)&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The Internet Architecture Board (IAB) is a technical body under ISOC responsible for overseeing the development of internet protocols and architecture."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-c4a2f7654873f1e1c6f37434762f36ae</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 7}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 35}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;INTERNET ENGINEERING TASK FORCE (IETF)&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The Internet Engineering Task Force (IETF) is a forum under IAB composed of working groups focused on short- to mid-term engineering issues, particularly protocol development and standardization, managed by the Internet Engineering Steering Group (IESG)."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-c4a2f7654873f1e1c6f37434762f36ae</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 7}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 34}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;INTERNET RESEARCH TASK FORCE (IRTF)&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The Internet Research Task Force (IRTF) is a forum composed of Research Groups (RGs) that studies long-term issues related to internet protocols, applications, and architecture."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Internet Research Task Force (IRTF) is a research-oriented division under IAB dedicated to long-term research topics related to internet protocols, applications, and architecture."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-c4a2f7654873f1e1c6f37434762f36ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e7c449009dbc73e669666df32bd7aeb1</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 7}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 35}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;SHANGHAI&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"GEO"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Shanghai is a city in China that hosts 5 of the country's 32 Internet Exchange Points (IXPs) as of August 2020."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-c4a2f7654873f1e1c6f37434762f36ae</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 4}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 21}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;GUANGZHOU&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"GEO"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Guangzhou is a city in China that hosts 4 of the country's 32 Internet Exchange Points (IXPs) as of August 2020."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-c4a2f7654873f1e1c6f37434762f36ae</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 4}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 21}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;HONG KONG&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"GEO"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Hong Kong is a special administrative region of China that hosts 9 of the country's 32 Internet Exchange Points (IXPs) as of August 2020."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-c4a2f7654873f1e1c6f37434762f36ae</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 4}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 21}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;TAIWAN&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"GEO"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Taiwan is a region of China that hosts 8 of the country's 32 Internet Exchange Points (IXPs) as of August 2020."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-c4a2f7654873f1e1c6f37434762f36ae</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 4}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 21}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;FRANKFURT&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"GEO"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Frankfurt is a city in Germany where DE-CIX, one of the world’s largest Internet Exchange Points, is located and serves as a major European internet hub."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-c4a2f7654873f1e1c6f37434762f36ae</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;GERMANY&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"GEO"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Germany is a country in Europe where the DE-CIX Internet Exchange Point is located in Frankfurt."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-c4a2f7654873f1e1c6f37434762f36ae</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;WORLD WIDE WEB (WWW)&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The World Wide Web (WWW) is a pivotal technological event developed by CERN that catalyzed the internet's exponential growth in the 1990s by enabling widespread, user-friendly access to online information."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-c4a2f7654873f1e1c6f37434762f36ae</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;INTERNET STANDARDIZATION&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Internet Standardization refers to the ongoing process of developing and coordinating technical standards for internet protocols and infrastructure, crucial for ensuring compatibility, interoperability, and orderly technological evolution."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-c4a2f7654873f1e1c6f37434762f36ae</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;FORMATION OF ISOC&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The Formation of ISOC in 1992 marks the transition of internet governance from U.S. government oversight to an international, community-driven model aimed at promoting global internet development and coordination."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-c4a2f7654873f1e1c6f37434762f36ae</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 7}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 35}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;CONTENT PROVIDER&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A Content Provider is a company that delivers video content over the internet to users; unlike ISPs, it does not offer internet connectivity but operates its own dedicated network to efficiently serve large volumes of video traffic and connects directly to ISPs and IXPs."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-c4a2f7654873f1e1c6f37434762f36ae</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 4}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 23}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;INTERNET ENGINEERING STEERING GROUP (IESG)&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The Internet Engineering Steering Group (IESG) is the management body of the IETF, responsible for guiding working groups and overseeing the technical standards development process within the Internet Engineering Task Force."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Internet Engineering Steering Group (IESG) manages the work of Working Groups (WGs) within a forum focused on short- and medium-term engineering issues, particularly protocol development and standardization."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-c4a2f7654873f1e1c6f37434762f36ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e7c449009dbc73e669666df32bd7aeb1</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 7}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 34}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;WORKING GROUP (WG)&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A Working Group (WG) is a focused team within the IETF that addresses specific short- to mid-term engineering problems related to internet protocol development and standardization, organized under technical areas."&lt;SEP&gt;"A Working Group (WG) is a subgroup within the IETF forum focused on specific short- and medium-term engineering problems, particularly in protocol development and standardization."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-c4a2f7654873f1e1c6f37434762f36ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e7c449009dbc73e669666df32bd7aeb1</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 7}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 34}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;INTERNET RESEARCH STEERING GROUP (IRSG)&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The Internet Research Steering Group (IRSG) oversees the activities of the IRTF's research groups and guides long-term internet research initiatives."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-e7c449009dbc73e669666df32bd7aeb1</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 7}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 35}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;RFC (REQUEST FOR COMMENTS)&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"RFC is a publication process and series of documents used for proposing and gathering feedback on internet standards, protocols, and related technologies; not all RFCs become official standards."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-e7c449009dbc73e669666df32bd7aeb1</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 7}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 32}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;INTERNET DRAFT&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Internet Draft is the initial stage in the internet standardization process with a six-month validity period; it is not yet an RFC document."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-e7c449009dbc73e669666df32bd7aeb1</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 7}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 32}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;PROPOSED STANDARD&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Proposed Standard is the second stage in the internet standardization process, at which point a specification becomes an RFC document and is open for community review and implementation."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-e7c449009dbc73e669666df32bd7aeb1</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 7}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 33}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;INTERNET STANDARD&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Internet Standard is the final and most rigorous stage of the standardization process, assigned a unique STD number after long-term validation; only a small fraction of RFCs achieve this status."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-e7c449009dbc73e669666df32bd7aeb1</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 7}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 33}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;RESEARCH GROUP (RG)&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A Research Group (RG) is a subgroup within the IRTF dedicated to investigating long-term research topics related to internet protocols, architecture, and applications."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-e7c449009dbc73e669666df32bd7aeb1</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 7}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 35}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;IETF FORUM&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The IETF Forum refers to the overarching organizational structure composed of multiple Working Groups (WGs) that coordinate internet engineering standardization efforts under IESG management."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-e7c449009dbc73e669666df32bd7aeb1</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 7}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 34}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;STD92&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"STD92 is the highest-numbered official Internet Standard as of November 2019, representing a finalized and widely validated protocol or specification within the RFC series."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-e7c449009dbc73e669666df32bd7aeb1</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 7}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 33}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;RFC 6410&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"RFC 6410 is a specific Request for Comments document published in October 2011 that formally eliminated the 'Draft Standard' stage from the Internet standardization process."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-e7c449009dbc73e669666df32bd7aeb1</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 7}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 33}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;HISTORICAL RFC&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Historical RFCs are documents that are either obsolete due to newer specifications or never achieved sufficient maturity to become standards; they are retained for reference."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-e7c449009dbc73e669666df32bd7aeb1</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 7}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 32}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;EXPERIMENTAL RFC&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Experimental RFCs describe protocols or technologies under active experimentation and are not intended for deployment in operational internet services."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-e7c449009dbc73e669666df32bd7aeb1</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 7}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 32}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;INFORMATIONAL RFC&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Informational RFCs provide general, historical, or instructional content about the Internet and are not part of the standards track."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-e7c449009dbc73e669666df32bd7aeb1</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 7}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 32}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;RFC INDEX&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The RFC Index is a continuously updated reference file that catalogs all published RFC documents with metadata such as title, publication date, category, and update relationships."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-e7c449009dbc73e669666df32bd7aeb1</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 7}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 32}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;GOOGLE&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Google is a multinational technology company that owns over 100 data centers globally, including 15 large ones each containing more than 100,000 servers, supporting internet-based services like information retrieval, web browsing, and video playback."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-30cc6309bbf7dc040e88d043d89a8722</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 5}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 25}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;BAIDU&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Baidu is a Chinese technology company that operates a major data center in Yangquan, Shanxi, which houses 160,000 servers and supports online services such as search and media streaming."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-30cc6309bbf7dc040e88d043d89a8722</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 5}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 26}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;YANGQUAN&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"GEO"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Yangquan is a city in Shanxi Province, China, where Baidu has constructed a large data center with 160,000 servers."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-30cc6309bbf7dc040e88d043d89a8722</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 5}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 26}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;EDGE PART OF THE INTERNET&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CONCEPT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Although not in the requested entity types, this is part of the Internet structure; however, per instructions, only organization, person, geo, or event are allowed. Since 'Internet' itself qualifies as a large-scale technological event/system facilitating global communication, it is captured as an event."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-30cc6309bbf7dc040e88d043d89a8722</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;CORE PART OF THE INTERNET&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The core part of the Internet refers to the infrastructure of networks and routers that provide connectivity and packet switching services to the edge; as a functional component of the global Internet event, it is subsumed under the broader 'INTERNET' entity."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-30cc6309bbf7dc040e88d043d89a8722</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;SERVER&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"In network communication, a server is an application process that provides services to clients. It runs continuously on a host machine (often called a 'server machine' or 'server-side'), waits passively for incoming requests, and can handle multiple client requests simultaneously. It typically requires robust hardware and advanced operating systems."&lt;SEP&gt;"While a server is technically a machine, in this context large collections of servers form part of organizational infrastructure; however, per entity types, 'server' alone isn't an organization. But since the text refers to servers owned by organizations like Google and Baidu, no standalone 'Server' entity is created. Instead, emphasis remains on owning organizations."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7076395e2a9a9ea6cdf6d289a0503201&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30cc6309bbf7dc040e88d043d89a8722</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;SCHOOL&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Schools are mentioned as possible owners of end systems (hosts) connected to the Internet, representing institutional users of internet services."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-30cc6309bbf7dc040e88d043d89a8722</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 5}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 25}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;GOVERNMENT AGENCY&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Government agencies are listed as potential owners of end systems (hosts) on the Internet, indicating their role as institutional participants in the network."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-30cc6309bbf7dc040e88d043d89a8722</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 5}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 25}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;CLIENT&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CONCEPT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"In the context of network communication, a client refers to an application process that requests services from a server. It runs on a host machine (sometimes called a 'client machine' or 'client-side'), initiates communication, and must know the server's address. The client is distinct from the human user and represents the service-requesting side in a client-server model."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7076395e2a9a9ea6cdf6d289a0503201</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;PEER-TO-PEER (P2P)&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CONCEPT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Peer-to-Peer (P2P) is a communication model where each host can act as both a client and a server. In this decentralized approach, connected machines exchange services or data directly without a fixed distinction between service requester and provider. P2P supports large-scale simultaneous users and underlies systems like file-sharing networks."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7076395e2a9a9ea6cdf6d289a0503201</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;HOST C&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"GEO"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Host C is a networked computer system that operates as a server host, hosting multiple server processes (e.g., Server 1 and Server 2) simultaneously. It provides services to client processes on Host A and Host B, exemplifying the capability of servers to handle numerous concurrent client connections."&lt;SEP&gt;"Host C is one of the participating machines in a peer-to-peer (P2P) network, capable of both requesting and providing services, exemplifying the dual client-server role in P2P communication."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-ad6543c9c55fc16571200afb61505ac0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7076395e2a9a9ea6cdf6d289a0503201</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 4}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 24}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;HOST D&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"GEO"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Host D is a node in the P2P network described in Figure 1-6 that engages in symmetric communication with other hosts like C and F, functioning interchangeably as client or server."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7076395e2a9a9ea6cdf6d289a0503201</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 4}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 24}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;HOST E&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"GEO"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Host E is a participant in the peer-to-peer network, running P2P software to enable direct data exchange with other peers such as F, without centralized control."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7076395e2a9a9ea6cdf6d289a0503201</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 4}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 24}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;HOST F&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"GEO"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Host F is a machine in the P2P setup that communicates equally with hosts like C and E, demonstrating the decentralized nature of peer-to-peer connections."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7076395e2a9a9ea6cdf6d289a0503201</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 4}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 24}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;FIGURE 1-5&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Figure 1-5 is a diagrammatic event or representation illustrating the client-server working mode, showing Host A as client and Host B as server in a network communication scenario."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7076395e2a9a9ea6cdf6d289a0503201</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;FIGURE 1-6&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Figure 1-6 is a visual depiction of the peer-to-peer (P2P) working mode, showing interconnected hosts (C, D, E, F) engaging in symmetric communication."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7076395e2a9a9ea6cdf6d289a0503201</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;CUSTOMER-SERVER COMMUNICATION&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Customer-Server Communication (more accurately Client-Server Communication) is the structured interaction event where a client initiates a service request and a server responds by providing the requested service over a network."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7076395e2a9a9ea6cdf6d289a0503201</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 4}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 22}, {"level": 2, "cluster": 54}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;PEER-TO-PEER COMMUNICATION&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Peer-to-Peer Communication is an event wherein two or more hosts establish equal-status connections to exchange data or services directly, without fixed client or server roles."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7076395e2a9a9ea6cdf6d289a0503201</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 4}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 24}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;NETWORK CORE&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"GEO"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The network core refers to the central infrastructure of the Internet responsible for routing and forwarding data packets between hosts; it is functionally and conceptually a key geographic/logical region of the global network."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-ca0c22897ac4785fecc70607badaf9cb</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;NETWORK EDGE&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"GEO"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The network edge encompasses the hosts and end systems connected to the Internet, forming the periphery where users and devices reside."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-ca0c22897ac4785fecc70607badaf9cb</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;TELEPHONE EXCHANGE&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A telephone exchange (or switch) is a system that connects multiple telephone lines to enable communication across a network; exchanges are interconnected to form the global telecommunications infrastructure."&lt;SEP&gt;"Telephone exchanges (or telephone switches) are systems that connect multiple telephone lines dynamically, historically using circuit switching to enable communication among many users without direct physical links."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-eaddc23a827beb1a3d74ea7736adb0dc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca0c22897ac4785fecc70607badaf9cb</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 3}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 17}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;CIRCUIT SWITCHING&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Circuit switching is a communication method historically used in telephone networks where a dedicated physical path is established between two parties for the duration of a call."&lt;SEP&gt;"Circuit switching is a communication method that involves establishing a dedicated physical connection between two users for the duration of a phone call, reserving end-to-end resources until the call ends."&lt;SEP&gt;"Circuit switching is a communication method where a dedicated physical path is established between source and destination before data transmission begins, ensuring continuous bit stream transfer."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-1dbda231b683f35c9fc91e9cc64fa2b9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaddc23a827beb1a3d74ea7736adb0dc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca0c22897ac4785fecc70607badaf9cb</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 3}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 17}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;PACKET SWITCHING&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Packet switching is a data transmission method that divides messages into smaller units called packets, each with a header, and uses store-and-forward technology to efficiently share network resources."&lt;SEP&gt;"Packet switching is a data transmission method used in the Internet’s core, where messages are broken into packets and routed independently through routers."&lt;SEP&gt;"Packet switching is a modern data transmission method that breaks messages into smaller packets, routes them independently across a network using dynamic paths, and reassembles them at the destination, offering efficiency, flexibility, speed, and reliability."&lt;SEP&gt;"Packet switching is the fundamental data transmission method described, where data is broken into short packets that are stored and forwarded dynamically across network links without requiring end-to-end resource reservation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Packet switching is the process by which routers receive, store, examine headers, consult forwarding tables, and relay packets toward their destination through a series of hops across the internet core."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-ca0c22897ac4785fecc70607badaf9cb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b07dbbacc9a0b17e8854aaf7b97228e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79ac7779be8905f2233724f344677601&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dbda231b683f35c9fc91e9cc64fa2b9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaddc23a827beb1a3d74ea7736adb0dc</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 3}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 16}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;TELECOMMUNICATION NETWORK&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The global telecommunications network is a system composed of interconnected telephone exchanges that enables worldwide voice communication using circuit switching."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-eaddc23a827beb1a3d74ea7736adb0dc</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 3}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 18}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;LOCAL EXCHANGE&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The local exchange (or市话交换机 in the text) connects individual telephone users via dedicated user lines and serves as the access point to the broader telecom network."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-eaddc23a827beb1a3d74ea7736adb0dc</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;TOLL EXCHANGE&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Though not explicitly named, the text implies the existence of long-distance (长途电话交换机) exchanges that handle inter-city or international calls and connect with local exchanges via trunk lines."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-eaddc23a827beb1a3d74ea7736adb0dc</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;USER LINE&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"GEO"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The physical or logical connection between a telephone subscriber and their local telephone exchange; it is a dedicated analog line exclusively used by one user."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-eaddc23a827beb1a3d74ea7736adb0dc</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;TRUNK LINE&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"GEO"</data>
  <data key="d1">"High-capacity digital transmission lines connecting telephone exchanges; trunk lines carry many simultaneous calls and are shared among users, with each active call occupying one channel or 'circuit'."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-eaddc23a827beb1a3d74ea7736adb0dc</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 3}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 17}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;CALL SETUP&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The process in circuit switching where a caller dials a number, the network allocates resources, and a dedicated end-to-end connection is established upon the called party answering."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-eaddc23a827beb1a3d74ea7736adb0dc</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 3}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 17}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;CALL TEARDOWN&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The process at the end of a circuit-switched call where the connection is terminated and all reserved communication resources are released back to the network."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-eaddc23a827beb1a3d74ea7736adb0dc</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 3}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 17}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;BUSY SIGNAL&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"An audible tone indicating that the telecommunications network lacks sufficient resources to establish a requested call, requiring the user to hang up and retry later."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-eaddc23a827beb1a3d74ea7736adb0dc</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 3}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 18}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;MESSAGE&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"In the context of packet switching, a message refers to the complete block of data to be transmitted, which is segmented into smaller units called packets for efficient network transmission."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-eaddc23a827beb1a3d74ea7736adb0dc</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 3}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 16}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;PACKET&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A formatted unit of data in packet switching, consisting of a payload (a segment of the original message) and a header containing control information for routing and reassembly."&lt;SEP&gt;"A packet (or分组) is the fundamental unit of data transmission in the internet, formed by adding a header containing control information—such as source and destination addresses—to a data segment. Packets are independently routed through the network."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-b07dbbacc9a0b17e8854aaf7b97228e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaddc23a827beb1a3d74ea7736adb0dc</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 3}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 16}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;ROUTING TABLE&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A routing table (or转发表) is a dynamic data structure maintained by routers that maps destination addresses to appropriate outgoing interfaces, updated through continuous exchange of routing information among routers."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-b07dbbacc9a0b17e8854aaf7b97228e2</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 5}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 25}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;ROUTER A&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Router A is a core network node that receives packets from host H₁, stores them temporarily in memory, consults its forwarding table, and forwards them either to router C or router B depending on network conditions."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-79ac7779be8905f2233724f344677601</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;ROUTER B&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Router B is an alternative routing node in the network that can receive packets from router A and forward them to router E when the primary path (A–C) experiences congestion."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-79ac7779be8905f2233724f344677601</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;ROUTER C&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Router C is an intermediate router that receives packets from router A and forwards them toward router E based on its forwarding table."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-79ac7779be8905f2233724f344677601</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;ROUTER E&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Router E is a router near the destination that receives packets from either router C or router B and delivers them to host H₅."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-79ac7779be8905f2233724f344677601</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;HOST H₁&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Host H₁ is the source endpoint initiating data transmission to host H₅ by sending packets one by one to its directly connected router, Router A."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-79ac7779be8905f2233724f344677601</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;HOST H₅&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Host H₅ is the destination endpoint that ultimately receives packets forwarded through the network via routers A, C (or B), and E."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-79ac7779be8905f2233724f344677601</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;HOST H₂&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Host H₂ is another source host capable of simultaneous communication with host H₆ via routers B and E."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-79ac7779be8905f2233724f344677601</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;HOST H₆&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Host H₆ is a destination host that communicates with host H₂ over a path involving routers B and E."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-79ac7779be8905f2233724f344677601</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;INTERNET CORE&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"GEO"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The Internet core refers to the central part of the internet composed of interconnected routers and high-speed links, often arranged in a mesh topology for reliability and efficiency."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-79ac7779be8905f2233724f344677601</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 3}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 16}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;LINK H₁-A&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"GEO"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Link H₁-A is the communication link between host H₁ and router A, used to transmit packets from the source host to the first router in the path."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-79ac7779be8905f2233724f344677601</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;LINK A-C&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"GEO"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Link A-C is a network link between router A and router C that carries forwarded packets under normal conditions but may become congested."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-79ac7779be8905f2233724f344677601</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;LINK A-B&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"GEO"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Link A-B is an alternative communication link from router A to router B, used when the primary path via link A-C is unavailable or congested."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-79ac7779be8905f2233724f344677601</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;LINK B-E&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"GEO"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Link B-E connects router B to router E and is part of the alternate route for traffic from H₁ to H₅, as well as the main route for H₂ to H₆."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-79ac7779be8905f2233724f344677601</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;LINK C-E&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"GEO"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Link C-E is the connection between router C and router E through which packets are forwarded toward the destination host H₅."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-79ac7779be8905f2233724f344677601</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;LINK E-H₅&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"GEO"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Link E-H₅ is the final communication link that delivers packets from router E directly to destination host H₅."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-79ac7779be8905f2233724f344677601</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;LINK E-H₆&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"GEO"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Link E-H₆ is the final link delivering packets from router E to host H₆ in the H₂–H₆ communication scenario."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-79ac7779be8905f2233724f344677601</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;FORWARDING TABLE&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The forwarding table is a data structure within each router (e.g., A, B, C, E) used to determine the next hop for incoming packets based on their destination."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-79ac7779be8905f2233724f344677601</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;ROUTING PROTOCOL&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The routing protocol is a distributed algorithm running in routers that dynamically computes optimal paths and adapts to network failures or congestion, ensuring reliable packet delivery."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-79ac7779be8905f2233724f344677601</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;MESH TOPOLOGY&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"GEO"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Mesh topology describes the physical and logical interconnection structure of the internet core, where multiple redundant paths between nodes enhance reliability and fault tolerance."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-79ac7779be8905f2233724f344677601</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;STORE-AND-FORWARD MECHANISM&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Store-and-forward mechanism is the process by which routers temporarily hold incoming packets in memory, look up the forwarding table, and then transmit them on an outgoing link."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-79ac7779be8905f2233724f344677601</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;BURSTY DATA TRAFFIC&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Bursty data traffic refers to the irregular, intermittent nature of computer data transmissions, which packet switching efficiently accommodates through dynamic resource allocation."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-79ac7779be8905f2233724f344677601</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;MESSAGE SWITCHING&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Message switching is a store-and-forward communication technique used historically in telegraph networks during the 1940s, where entire messages are received, stored, and then forwarded based on destination address."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-1dbda231b683f35c9fc91e9cc64fa2b9</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 3}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 19}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;POSTAL COMMUNICATION&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Postal communication is cited as an ancient analog of store-and-forward principles, where messages are physically transported in discrete units through intermediate nodes over time."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-1dbda231b683f35c9fc91e9cc64fa2b9</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 3}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 16}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;TELEGRAPH COMMUNICATION&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Telegraph communication in the 1940s implemented message switching by receiving full messages as paper tapes, storing them, and manually forwarding them to the next station based on destination."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-1dbda231b683f35c9fc91e9cc64fa2b9</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 3}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 19}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;铁道部&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The Ministry of Railways was the first organization in China to build a dedicated wide-area computer network, beginning networking experiments as early as 1980."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-779592fbef47ea27eab563c9f2b5fb8a</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;CNPAC&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"CNPAC (China Public Packet Switched Data Network) was China's first public packet-switched network, launched in November 1989."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-779592fbef47ea27eab563c9f2b5fb8a</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;公安&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Public security departments in China established their own dedicated wide-area computer networks in the late 1980s for transmitting critical data."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-779592fbef47ea27eab563c9f2b5fb8a</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;军队&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The Chinese military established its own dedicated wide-area computer networks in the late 1980s for internal communications and data transfer."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-779592fbef47ea27eab563c9f2b5fb8a</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;中国科学院高能物理研究所&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The Institute of High Energy Physics of the Chinese Academy of Sciences set up China's first World Wide Web server in May 1994."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-779592fbef47ea27eab563c9f2b5fb8a</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;CHINANET&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"CHINANET (China Public Computer Internet) is one of China's largest nationwide public computer networks, officially launched in September 1994 and operated by China Telecom."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-779592fbef47ea27eab563c9f2b5fb8a</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;中国电信互联网 CHINANET&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Also known as China Telecom Internet, it is the rebranded name of the original China Public Computer Internet (CHINANET), forming one of the five major national internet backbones in China."</data>
