  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Alibaba.com is a business-to-business e-commerce platform founded by Jack Ma in 1999, serving as the cornerstone of Alibaba Group’s global trade initiatives."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-3cf7f9315ae398e3c73bd500746d26d8</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;ALIPAY.COM&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Alipay.com is the domain and operational face of Alipay, Alibaba Group’s third-party online payment platform launched in 2004 to facilitate secure e-commerce transactions in China."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-3cf7f9315ae398e3c73bd500746d26d8</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;WECHAT&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"WeChat is the international version of Weixin, developed by Tencent and led by Zhang Xiaolong, offering slightly different features than the domestic Chinese version but sharing core functionalities like messaging and payments."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-3cf7f9315ae398e3c73bd500746d26d8</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;TELECOMMUNICATION COMPANIES&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Telecommunication companies, whether state-owned or private, build and operate public networks that any user can access by paying fees according to the company's regulations."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-982f03b0a500a9e6bd7272aaa9e050d8</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 5}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 31}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;MILITARY&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The military operates a private network designed to meet its specific operational and communication needs, not providing services to external entities."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-982f03b0a500a9e6bd7272aaa9e050d8</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 2}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;RAILWAY SYSTEM&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The railway system maintains its own private network to support its specialized business operations, restricted to internal use only."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-982f03b0a500a9e6bd7272aaa9e050d8</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 2}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;BANKING SYSTEM&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Banks utilize dedicated private networks tailored to their unique business requirements, with no external access permitted."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-982f03b0a500a9e6bd7272aaa9e050d8</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 2}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;POWER SYSTEM&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The power (electricity) sector runs a private network exclusively for its internal operational purposes, not available to outside users."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-982f03b0a500a9e6bd7272aaa9e050d8</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 2}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;WIDE AREA NETWORK (WAN)&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"GEO"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A Wide Area Network spans large geographical distances, such as across countries, forming the core of the internet by transporting data between hosts over high-capacity, high-speed links."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-982f03b0a500a9e6bd7272aaa9e050d8</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 5}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 31}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;METROPOLITAN AREA NETWORK (MAN)&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"GEO"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A Metropolitan Area Network covers a city-scale area (approximately 5–50 km), interconnecting multiple local area networks and may be owned by one or more organizations or operated as public infrastructure."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-982f03b0a500a9e6bd7272aaa9e050d8</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 4}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 20}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;LOCAL AREA NETWORK (LAN)&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"GEO"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A Local Area Network is geographically confined to a small area (around 1 km), typically within a school, factory, campus, or enterprise, connecting microcomputers or workstations via high-speed communication lines."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-982f03b0a500a9e6bd7272aaa9e050d8</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 4}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 20}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;PERSONAL AREA NETWORK (PAN)&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"GEO"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A Personal Area Network operates within a very limited range (about 10 meters), wirelessly connecting personal electronic devices like laptops in an individual’s workspace."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-982f03b0a500a9e6bd7272aaa9e050d8</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;ACCESS NETWORK (AN)&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"GEO"</data>
  <data key="d1">"An Access Network, also known as a local or residential access network, spans from a few hundred meters to several kilometers, serving as the physical bridge between end-user systems and the internet through a local ISP’s infrastructure."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-982f03b0a500a9e6bd7272aaa9e050d8</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 4}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 23}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;PUBLIC NETWORK&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"GEO"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A public network is a large-scale telecommunications infrastructure built by telecom companies that is accessible to any user willing to pay the required fees, serving as a shared communication resource."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-982f03b0a500a9e6bd7272aaa9e050d8</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 5}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 31}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;PRIVATE NETWORK&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"GEO"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A private network is a dedicated communications infrastructure built by an organization (e.g., military, bank, railway) exclusively for its internal use and not available to external parties."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-982f03b0a500a9e6bd7272aaa9e050d8</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 2}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;CAMPUS NETWORK&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"GEO"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A campus network refers to multiple interconnected local area networks within a school or university, forming a larger LAN-based infrastructure."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-982f03b0a500a9e6bd7272aaa9e050d8</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 4}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 20}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;ENTERPRISE NETWORK&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"GEO"</data>
  <data key="d1">"An enterprise network consists of multiple interconnected local area networks within a business or corporate environment, enabling internal communication and resource sharing."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-982f03b0a500a9e6bd7272aaa9e050d8</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 4}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 20}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;BIT&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CONCEPT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Bit is the fundamental unit of information in information theory and digital communications, used to quantify data."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-a2ab20554e89073ba513e052dbf5667c</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;DATA RATE&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CONCEPT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Data rate, also known as bit rate, refers to the speed at which data is transmitted in a network, measured in bits per second (bit/s)."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-a2ab20554e89073ba513e052dbf5667c</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;BANDWIDTH&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CONCEPT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Bandwidth (data rate) is the maximum rate of data transfer across a network path; used in delay and bit-in-flight calculations throughout the text."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bandwidth has dual meanings: (1) in signal processing, it is the frequency range of a signal; (2) in computer networking, it denotes the maximum data transfer capacity of a channel, measured in bit/s."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-a2ab20554e89073ba513e052dbf5667c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b44f82ed51f5dd17e63648288cc859c4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;THROUGHPUT&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CONCEPT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Throughput is the actual amount of data successfully transmitted through a network over a given time period, often lower than the nominal bandwidth due to congestion or limitations."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-a2ab20554e89073ba513e052dbf5667c</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;SERVER B&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Server B is a network server connected to the Internet at 1 Gbit/s, serving content such as video streams to multiple users."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-a2ab20554e89073ba513e052dbf5667c</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 4}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 22}, {"level": 2, "cluster": 54}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;主机 A&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
  <data key="d1">"主机 A (Host A) is referred to as a user endpoint experiencing potential throughput reduction due to internet congestion; though technically a device, in this context it represents the user's perspective and is treated as a personified entity expressing user concerns."&lt;SEP&gt;"主机 A is a source computing device that sends data or files to 主机 B across network links in multiple networking scenarios described in the text."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-0174bb3213f0a9872650fbd9b001dae3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c24d88fe5e8a41451cb6e5c8bf5bb54c</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 10}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 44}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;路由器&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Routers are networking devices that forward data packets across the internet; they are conceptualized here as organizational entities managing traffic flow, particularly in contexts like queuing and forwarding decisions."&lt;SEP&gt;"The router is an intermediary networking device that receives incoming data from Host 1, processes it up to the network layer, determines the forwarding path using its routing table, and retransmits the data toward Host 2."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d13275eb4af2ef9a790f16e7564a101b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0174bb3213f0a9872650fbd9b001dae3</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;光纤&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"GEO"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Optical fiber is a physical transmission medium used in internet infrastructure; given the allowed types, it is classified as a geographic entity representing a key component of the network's physical layer."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-0174bb3213f0a9872650fbd9b001dae3</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;铜线电缆&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"GEO"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Copper cable is another physical transmission medium referenced in the text for signal propagation; categorized as a geo-type entity due to its role as part of the physical network geography."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-0174bb3213f0a9872650fbd9b001dae3</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;收费站比喻事件&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The tollbooth analogy is a didactic event used to illustrate the difference between transmission delay and propagation delay, involving 10 cars, a 50 km journey, and time calculations."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-0174bb3213f0a9872650fbd9b001dae3</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;总时延计算&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The summation of four distinct delay components (sending, propagation, processing, and queuing) into total network delay is described as a key conceptual event in understanding network performance."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-0174bb3213f0a9872650fbd9b001dae3</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;排队时延溢出事件&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"When network traffic is heavy, router queues can overflow, causing packet loss—this scenario is described as an event equivalent to infinite queuing delay."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-0174bb3213f0a9872650fbd9b001dae3</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;NETWORK&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"GEO"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The term 'network' refers broadly to a communication infrastructure spanning geographic space, through which data is transmitted; in this context, it functions as a geographically distributed system subject to various transmission delays."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-5b9b9cae118d88a7180e9150d774bb1b</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;FIBER OPTIC LINK&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"GEO"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A 1000 km fiber optic link is explicitly mentioned as the medium for data transmission between two distant computers, representing a physical geographic communication pathway."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-5b9b9cae118d88a7180e9150d774bb1b</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;COMPUTER&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"GEO"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A destination computer located 1000 km away via fiber is referenced as the endpoint of data transmission, functioning as a geographic node in the network."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-5b9b9cae118d88a7180e9150d774bb1b</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;KEYBOARD&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"GEO"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The keyboard is mentioned as the source of a single-byte input (e.g., a typed character), representing a physical input device at a specific location in the communication chain."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-5b9b9cae118d88a7180e9150d774bb1b</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;HIGH-SPEED LINK&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"GEO"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Referred to in the discussion of bandwidth and transmission, a high-speed (or high-bandwidth) link denotes a physical or logical segment of the network infrastructure with elevated data sending capacity."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-5b9b9cae118d88a7180e9150d774bb1b</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;COMMUNICATION LINE&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"GEO"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The communication line (e.g., fiber) is described as the medium where electromagnetic waves propagate at near light speed; its material properties determine propagation delay, making it a geographic/physical entity."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-5b9b9cae118d88a7180e9150d774bb1b</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;FIGURE 1-12&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Figure 1-12 is an illustrative event in the text that visually distinguishes where different types of delays occur in a network, serving as a pedagogical milestone for understanding delay components."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-5b9b9cae118d88a7180e9150d774bb1b</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;DATA TRANSMISSION EXAMPLE&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The example involving transmission of a 100 MB block over a 1 Mbit/s channel is a conceptual event used to demonstrate how send delay dominates total delay under certain conditions."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-5b9b9cae118d88a7180e9150d774bb1b</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;SINGLE-BYTE TRANSMISSION EXAMPLE&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The scenario of sending one byte (e.g., from a keyboard) illustrates a contrasting case where propagation delay dominates, forming a distinct analytical event in the explanation."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-5b9b9cae118d88a7180e9150d774bb1b</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;TOTAL DELAY CALCULATION&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The derivation and discussion of total delay as the sum of four component delays (send, propagation, processing, queuing) constitutes a key conceptual event in the text."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-5b9b9cae118d88a7180e9150d774bb1b</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;光纤信道&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"GEO"</data>
  <data key="d1">"光纤信道 refers to an optical fiber transmission medium used in data communication, characterized by high data sending rates but slightly lower propagation speed compared to copper wires."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-dd3fe3d1c11062f19012f7da1f7eaf5c</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;铜线&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"GEO"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A physical transmission medium, such as Category 5 twisted-pair cable, used for data transmission; electromagnetic signals propagate through it at approximately 231,000 km/s."&lt;SEP&gt;"铜线 (copper wire), such as Category 5 cable, is a physical transmission medium where electromagnetic waves propagate at approximately 231,000 km/s, faster than light in optical fiber."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-dd3fe3d1c11062f19012f7da1f7eaf5c</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;时延带宽积&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CONCEPT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Though not in the requested entity types, this is a key networking metric defined as the product of propagation delay and bandwidth, representing the number of bits that can be 'in flight' on a link. However, per instructions, only organization, person, geo, and event are valid types—so this should not be included."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-dd3fe3d1c11062f19012f7da1f7eaf5c</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;卫星通信&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"GEO"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Satellite communication refers to a geographically distributed communication system involving ground stations and orbiting satellites, introducing significant propagation delays due to long distances; it constitutes a spatially extended network infrastructure."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-dd3fe3d1c11062f19012f7da1f7eaf5c</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;RTT&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CONCEPT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"RTT (Round-Trip Time) is a key performance metric in computer networking representing the total time for a signal to travel to a destination and back, including processing, queuing, and transmission delays—especially significant in satellite communications."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-febb982eeee1d6798571d55df8b4d130</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;BANDWIDTH-DELAY PRODUCT&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CONCEPT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The bandwidth-delay product is defined in the text as the product of bandwidth and RTT, representing the amount of data that can be 'in flight' before an acknowledgment is received; two definitions are noted, differing by a factor of two."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-febb982eeee1d6798571d55df8b4d130</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;CHANNEL UTILIZATION&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CONCEPT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Channel utilization measures the percentage of time a communication channel carries data; the text explains it is not ideal to maximize this metric due to resulting queueing delays."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-febb982eeee1d6798571d55df8b4d130</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;NETWORK UTILIZATION&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CONCEPT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Network utilization is described as the weighted average of all channel utilizations across a network, with higher values leading to exponentially increasing delays per the formula D = D₀/(1−U)."&lt;SEP&gt;"Network utilization refers to the degree to which network resources are being used; mentioned in question 1-15 with a value of 90% to estimate network delay."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-febb982eeee1d6798571d55df8b4d130&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b44f82ed51f5dd17e63648288cc859c4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;FORMULA (1-5)&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Formula (1-5), D = D₀/(1−U), models the relationship between current network delay (D), idle delay (D₀), and network utilization (U); it illustrates how delay grows asymptotically as utilization approaches 100%."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-febb982eeee1d6798571d55df8b4d130</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;FIGURE 1-14&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Figure 1-14 is a diagram illustrating the nonlinear relationship between network utilization and delay, referenced to support the theoretical discussion of queuing effects."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-febb982eeee1d6798571d55df8b4d130</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;SATELLITE COMMUNICATION&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Satellite communication is mentioned as a scenario where RTT is notably long, making it a critical factor in network performance evaluation."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-febb982eeee1d6798571d55df8b4d130</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;IBM&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"IBM is a U.S. company that announced the System Network Architecture (SNA) in 1974, a layered network standard still used in proprietary networks built with IBM mainframes."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-8fe573a1edca72839801eaf0704a5d62</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATION FOR STANDARDIZATION (ISO)&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The International Organization for Standardization (ISO) is an international body that, in 1977, established a dedicated committee to develop a universal framework for interconnecting computer networks, resulting in the OSI Reference Model."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-8fe573a1edca72839801eaf0704a5d62</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 0}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 14}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;OPEN SYSTEMS INTERCONNECTION REFERENCE MODEL (OSI/RM)&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The Open Systems Interconnection Reference Model (OSI/RM) is a conceptual framework developed by ISO to enable global interoperability of computer networks through a standardized seven-layer architecture, formalized as ISO 7498 in 1983."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-8fe573a1edca72839801eaf0704a5d62</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 0}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 14}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;SYSTEM NETWORK ARCHITECTURE (SNA)&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"System Network Architecture (SNA) is a proprietary network architecture introduced by IBM in 1974 based on a layered design, primarily used in IBM mainframe environments."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-8fe573a1edca72839801eaf0704a5d62</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;IBM CORPORATION&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"IBM Corporation, also referred to as IBM, is a major American technology company that developed the proprietary System Network Architecture (SNA) for its mainframe systems."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-8fe573a1edca72839801eaf0704a5d62</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;ISO 7498&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"ISO 7498 is the formal international standard published in 1983 that defines the Open Systems Interconnection Basic Reference Model (OSI/RM), establishing the seven-layer network architecture."&lt;SEP&gt;"ISO 7498 is the official international standard document published in 1983 that codifies the OSI Reference Model, representing a formal milestone in global networking standardization."&lt;SEP&gt;"The formal international standard published by ISO in 1983 that defines the Open Systems Interconnection (OSI) basic reference model, commonly known as the seven-layer protocol architecture."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-8fe573a1edca72839801eaf0704a5d62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d24b4f467e6a4552877bf9e42c614b2</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;GLOBAL COMPUTER NETWORKS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CONCEPT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Although not an allowed entity type, this phrase reflects a collective idea; however, per instructions, only organization, person, geo, or event are valid. Thus, no entity extracted here."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-8fe573a1edca72839801eaf0704a5d62</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;WORLDWIDE TELECOMMUNICATIONS INFRASTRUCTURE&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CONCEPT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Referenced analogously to OSI (like telephone/postal systems), but not a valid entity type—no extraction."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-8fe573a1edca72839801eaf0704a5d62</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;ISO&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The International Organization for Standardization (ISO) is responsible for developing international standards, including the OSI reference model (ISO 7498), and later collaborated with CCITT on related standards."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7d24b4f467e6a4552877bf9e42c614b2</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 8}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 37}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;CCITT&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The International Telegraph and Telephone Consultative Committee (CCITT) originally focused on telecommunications standards and later jointly developed OSI-related standards with ISO, such as recommendation X.200."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7d24b4f467e6a4552877bf9e42c614b2</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 8}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 37}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;OSI&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"OSI (Open Systems Interconnection) is a reference model for network communication that defines layered protocols, service primitives, and data units such as SDU and PDU. It provides the framework for understanding layer-to-layer interactions in computer networks."&lt;SEP&gt;"OSI (Open Systems Interconnection) is an international standard-setting body that developed the seven-layer reference model for network communication protocols."&lt;SEP&gt;"OSI (Open Systems Interconnection) is referenced as the originator of the seven-layer protocol architecture model used in computer networking theory."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Open Systems Interconnection (OSI) initiative refers to the development and promotion of a seven-layer networking reference model in the 1980s, which aimed to standardize global computer network communication but ultimately failed in the marketplace despite theoretical success."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-e05b6bd70dc7a04d2758501d65696eff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9aad351f9f6f14230a142381a106309&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ffae866e92afc591d3a1c26480aeeb3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d24b4f467e6a4552877bf9e42c614b2</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 8}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 37}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATION FOR STANDARDIZATION&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The full name of ISO, an international body that develops and publishes global standards, including the OSI reference model (ISO 7498) in 1983."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7d24b4f467e6a4552877bf9e42c614b2</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;INTERNATIONAL TELEGRAPH AND TELEPHONE CONSULTATIVE COMMITTEE&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The full name of CCITT, a former standards organization focused on telecommunications, which later merged into ITU-T; it co-developed the OSI model with ISO through its X.200 recommendation."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7d24b4f467e6a4552877bf9e42c614b2</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;X.200&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A CCITT recommendation that describes the Open Systems Interconnection basic reference model, functionally identical to ISO 7498, developed jointly by CCITT and ISO."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7d24b4f467e6a4552877bf9e42c614b2</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;1980S&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The decade during which OSI gained significant support from major corporations and government agencies, with widespread expectation that it would become the global networking standard."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7d24b4f467e6a4552877bf9e42c614b2</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;1990S EARLY&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The period around the early 1990s when OSI was deemed a market failure despite having a complete suite of standards, as TCP/IP-based internet had already achieved broad operational success."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7d24b4f467e6a4552877bf9e42c614b2</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;主机 1&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"GEO"</data>
  <data key="d1">"主机 1 (Host 1) is a computer system involved in file transfer over a communication network, serving as the sending endpoint in the described example."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-a1b5e11d873f879fd9610fda65292066</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 0}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 13}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;主机 2&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"GEO"</data>
  <data key="d1">"主机 2 (Host 2) is a computer system involved in file transfer over a communication network, serving as the receiving endpoint in the described example."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-a1b5e11d873f879fd9610fda65292066</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 0}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 13}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;文件传送模块&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The file transfer module is a conceptual software component responsible for managing file transmission tasks, including ensuring the receiver is ready and handling file format conversion; it operates as the highest layer in the layered protocol example."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-a1b5e11d873f879fd9610fda65292066</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 0}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 13}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;通信服务模块&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The communication service module is a conceptual software layer that provides reliable exchange of files and commands between systems, serving as an intermediary layer used by higher-level applications like the file transfer or email modules."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-a1b5e11d873f879fd9610fda65292066</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 0}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 13}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;网络接入模块&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The network access module is a conceptual software layer responsible for handling low-level network interface details and providing services to upper layers to enable reliable communication."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-a1b5e11d873f879fd9610fda65292066</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 0}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 13}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;电子邮件模块&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The email module is a hypothetical application-layer component mentioned as an alternative to the file transfer module, capable of utilizing the same underlying communication service module for reliable data exchange."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-a1b5e11d873f879fd9610fda65292066</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 0}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 13}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;IETF&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The Internet Engineering Task Force (IETF) is an open standards organization that develops and promotes voluntary internet standards, particularly those comprising the TCP/IP protocol suite."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Internet Engineering Task Force (IETF) is an organization responsible for developing and promoting voluntary internet standards, particularly those comprising the TCP/IP protocol suite."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-4ffae866e92afc591d3a1c26480aeeb3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e05b6bd70dc7a04d2758501d65696eff</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 8}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 38}, {"level": 2, "cluster": 55}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;IEEE 802 COMMITTEE&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The IEEE 802 Committee is a working group under the Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers (IEEE) that develops standards for local area networks (LANs) and metropolitan area networks (MANs), including link-layer protocols."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-e05b6bd70dc7a04d2758501d65696eff</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;FIVE-LAYER PROTOCOL ARCHITECTURE&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The Five-Layer Protocol Architecture is an educational model combining features of OSI and TCP/IP models to simplify the teaching of computer networking principles."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-e05b6bd70dc7a04d2758501d65696eff</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 8}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 36}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;OSI SEVEN-LAYER MODEL&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The OSI Seven-Layer Model is a conceptual framework used to understand and implement network protocols in seven distinct layers, noted for its theoretical completeness but practical complexity."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-e05b6bd70dc7a04d2758501d65696eff</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 8}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 36}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;TCP/IP FOUR-LAYER MODEL&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The TCP/IP Four-Layer Model is a practical networking architecture consisting of the application, transport, internet, and link layers, widely used in real-world internet communications."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-e05b6bd70dc7a04d2758501d65696eff</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 8}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 36}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;RFC 1122&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"DOCUMENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"RFC 1122 is an Internet standard document (STD3) that defines the requirements for internet hosts and specifies the TCP/IP protocol architecture, including the definition of the link layer."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-e05b6bd70dc7a04d2758501d65696eff</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 8}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 36}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;STD3&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"DOCUMENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"STD3 refers to an official Internet Standard that includes RFC 1122 and outlines host requirements for communication layers in the TCP/IP model."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-e05b6bd70dc7a04d2758501d65696eff</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 8}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 36}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;IEEE&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers (IEEE) is the parent organization overseeing the IEEE 802 Committee, which develops LAN/MAN standards including those for the link layer."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-e05b6bd70dc7a04d2758501d65696eff</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;NETWORK ARCHITECTURE&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CONCEPT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Network architecture refers to the structured design of a computer network, defined as the collection of layers and protocols that specify functionalities without dictating implementation."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-e05b6bd70dc7a04d2758501d65696eff</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 8}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 36}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;LINK LAYER&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CONCEPT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The Link Layer (also called network interface layer or media access layer) is the lowest layer in the TCP/IP model, responsible for handling communication within a physical network segment and governed by IEEE 802 standards."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-e05b6bd70dc7a04d2758501d65696eff</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;APPLICATION LAYER&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CONCEPT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The Application Layer is the topmost layer in both TCP/IP and five-layer models, providing services directly to user applications such as email, file transfer, and web browsing."&lt;SEP&gt;"The topmost layer in the five-layer network protocol model, responsible for providing network services directly to end-user applications."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d8da4662c1f51095b9efcbe2f2cbe556&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e05b6bd70dc7a04d2758501d65696eff</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 8}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 36}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;TRANSPORT LAYER&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CONCEPT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The Transport Layer ensures end-to-end communication, handles error recovery, and manages flow control between hosts; it includes protocols like TCP and UDP."&lt;SEP&gt;"The transport layer in the network protocol stack ensures end-to-end communication, with key protocols being TCP and UDP."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d8da4662c1f51095b9efcbe2f2cbe556&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e05b6bd70dc7a04d2758501d65696eff</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 8}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 38}, {"level": 2, "cluster": 56}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;INTERNET LAYER&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CONCEPT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The Internet Layer (or Network Layer in some models) is responsible for logical addressing and routing across interconnected networks, primarily implemented via the IP protocol."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-e05b6bd70dc7a04d2758501d65696eff</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 8}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 36}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;IP (INTERNET PROTOCOL)&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"IP is the core network-layer protocol of the Internet, responsible for addressing and routing packets across heterogeneous networks; it defines the structure and behavior of the network (or internetwork) layer."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-1c827098a559a559af3c8df103949b45</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 5}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 28}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;TCP/IP PROTOCOL SUITE&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The TCP/IP Protocol Suite is a structured set of communication protocols forming the foundation of the Internet. It is characterized by a sandglass-shaped architecture with a narrow IP layer at its core, supporting multiple transport protocols above and running over diverse network technologies below. This design enables universal interoperability and scalability."&lt;SEP&gt;"The TCP/IP protocol suite is the collection of communication protocols used by the Internet, with TCP and IP being the most fundamental; it encompasses multiple layers and defines how data is transmitted end-to-end."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-1c827098a559a559af3c8df103949b45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ad6543c9c55fc16571200afb61505ac0</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 5}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 28}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;APPLICATION PROCESS AP₁&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
  <data key="d1">"AP₁ is the source application process on Host 1 that initiates data transmission to AP₂; though abstract, it acts as an agent in the communication flow and is personified as an initiating entity."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-1c827098a559a559af3c8df103949b45</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 5}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 29}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;APPLICATION PROCESS AP₂&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
  <data key="d1">"AP₂ is the destination application process on Host 2 that receives data from AP₁; like AP₁, it serves as a terminal endpoint in the communication and is treated as a personified recipient."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-1c827098a559a559af3c8df103949b45</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 5}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 29}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;OSI参考模型&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The OSI reference model is a conceptual framework used to describe the functions of a networking or telecommunication system, structured into seven layers that standardize communication protocols."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d13275eb4af2ef9a790f16e7564a101b</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;主机1&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"GEO"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Host 1 is the source computing device from which data originates and is transmitted through the network layers toward a destination."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d13275eb4af2ef9a790f16e7564a101b</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;主机2&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"GEO"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Host 2 is the destination computing device that receives data transmitted through the network, where the data is processed up through the protocol stack to the application layer."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d13275eb4af2ef9a790f16e7564a101b</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;应用进程 AP₁&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Application Process AP₁ is a software entity on Host 1 that initiates data transmission by passing data down through the protocol stack; it operates under the illusion of direct communication with AP₂."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d13275eb4af2ef9a790f16e7564a101b</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;应用进程 AP₂&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Application Process AP₂ is a software entity on Host 2 that receives the transmitted data after it has been processed up through the protocol stack; it appears to directly receive data from AP₁."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d13275eb4af2ef9a790f16e7564a101b</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;协议栈&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The protocol stack refers to the layered architecture of communication protocols, analogous to a stack structure, where each layer provides services to the layer above and uses services from the layer below."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d13275eb4af2ef9a790f16e7564a101b</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;对等层&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Peer layers are corresponding layers in two communicating systems (e.g., Layer 4 in Host 1 and Layer 4 in Host 2) that exchange protocol data units according to agreed-upon rules, enabling logical horizontal communication."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d13275eb4af2ef9a790f16e7564a101b</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;实体&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
  <data key="d1">"In networking context, an 'entity' denotes any hardware or software process capable of sending or receiving information; often implemented as a software module within a specific layer of a protocol stack."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d13275eb4af2ef9a790f16e7564a101b</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;协议&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A protocol is a set of rules governing communication between peer entities, defining both syntax (data format) and semantics (actions like retransmission or discard) for reliable data exchange."&lt;SEP&gt;"Protocol is discussed extensively but is a concept, not an organization, person, geo, or event—thus excluded per entity type constraints."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d13275eb4af2ef9a790f16e7564a101b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9aad351f9f6f14230a142381a106309</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;服务&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CONCEPT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Service refers to the visible functionality provided by one layer to the layer above it through a vertical interface; only functions observable by the upper layer qualify as services."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d13275eb4af2ef9a790f16e7564a101b</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;服务访问点&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A Service Access Point (SAP) is the interface location where one layer provides services to the adjacent higher layer, enabling inter-layer communication within a single system."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d13275eb4af2ef9a790f16e7564a101b</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;服务原语&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Service primitives are commands used in the OSI model for communication between adjacent layers, allowing upper layers to request or respond to services from lower layers."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d13275eb4af2ef9a790f16e7564a101b</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;PDU&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Protocol Data Unit (PDU) is the unit of data specified in a protocol of a given layer, consisting of control information and user data, exchanged between peer layers in the OSI model."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d13275eb4af2ef9a790f16e7564a101b</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;蓝军 1&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"蓝军 1 is a military or strategic unit involved in a coordination scenario where it must confirm receipt of messages before initiating an attack. It participates in an infinite confirmation loop with 蓝军 2."&lt;SEP&gt;"蓝军 1 is a military unit stationed on the eastern hilltop, engaged in coordinated combat planning with 蓝军 2 against 白军. It initiates communication to propose a joint attack but faces uncertainty due to unreliable communication channels."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-77d64847854ffc2a5d57b6da6e7bc7d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9aad351f9f6f14230a142381a106309</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 6}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;蓝军 2&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"蓝军 2 is a military unit stationed on the western hilltop, collaborating with 蓝军 1 to defeat 白军. It responds to attack proposals with confirmations but remains hesitant to act without assurance that its messages were received."&lt;SEP&gt;"蓝军 2 is another military or strategic unit coordinating with 蓝军 1, also caught in an infinite loop of mutual confirmations regarding message receipt, preventing decisive action."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-77d64847854ffc2a5d57b6da6e7bc7d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9aad351f9f6f14230a142381a106309</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 6}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;白军&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"白军 is a military force stationed in the valley, stronger than either 蓝军 1 or 蓝军 2 individually but vulnerable to their coordinated attack."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-b9aad351f9f6f14230a142381a106309</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 6}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;协同作战&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"协同作战 (coordinated attack) is a planned joint military action between 蓝军 1 and 蓝军 2 scheduled for noon the following day, intended to defeat 白军. Its success depends on reliable communication and mutual confirmation between the two blue armies."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-b9aad351f9f6f14230a142381a106309</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 6}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;服务访问点SAP&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CONCEPT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Although not in the requested entity types, note: however, per instructions only [organization,person,geo,event] are valid. Since SAP is an abstract interface and not an organization, person, geo, or event, it is excluded."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-b9aad351f9f6f14230a142381a106309</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;服务数据单元SDU&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CONCEPT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"SDU is a technical term in the OSI model; however, it does not belong to any of the allowed entity types [organization,person,geo,event], so it is not included as an entity."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-b9aad351f9f6f14230a142381a106309</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;东山顶&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"GEO"</data>
  <data key="d1">"东山顶 (Eastern mountaintop) is the geographic location where 蓝军 1 is stationed during the military scenario."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-b9aad351f9f6f14230a142381a106309</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;西山顶&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"GEO"</data>
  <data key="d1">"西山顶 (Western mountaintop) is the geographic location where 蓝军 2 is stationed during the military scenario."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-b9aad351f9f6f14230a142381a106309</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;山谷&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"GEO"</data>
  <data key="d1">"山谷 (Valley) is the geographic location where 白军 is encamped, situated between the two mountaintops occupied by the blue armies."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-b9aad351f9f6f14230a142381a106309</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;无限循环的协议&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"无限循环的协议 refers to the communication scenario between 蓝军 1 and 蓝军 2 where each side continuously waits for confirmation of receipt, leading to an unresolvable coordination problem."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-77d64847854ffc2a5d57b6da6e7bc7d1</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;TCP/IP 的体系结构&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"TCP/IP 的体系结构 describes the conceptual framework and layered design of the TCP/IP protocol suite, discussed in terms of its four-layer model and alternative representations."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-77d64847854ffc2a5d57b6da6e7bc7d1</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;图 1-19&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"图 1-19 is a diagram illustrating the infinite loop of acknowledgments between 蓝军 1 and 蓝军 2, symbolizing the impossibility of achieving guaranteed coordination under the given protocol constraints."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-77d64847854ffc2a5d57b6da6e7bc7d1</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;图 1-20&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"图 1-20 is a visual representation of the standard four-layer TCP/IP protocol architecture, used to explain its simplified structure compared to OSI."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-77d64847854ffc2a5d57b6da6e7bc7d1</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;图 1-21&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"图 1-21 depicts an evolved view of the TCP/IP architecture where some applications bypass transport layers and interact directly with IP or link layers."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-77d64847854ffc2a5d57b6da6e7bc7d1</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;图 1-22&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"图 1-22 illustrates an alternative沙漏-shaped representation of the TCP/IP protocol suite, emphasizing multiple protocols at the application and network interface layers converging through a narrow IP layer."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-77d64847854ffc2a5d57b6da6e7bc7d1</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;CLIENT PROCESS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A Client Process is an application-layer software entity running on a host (such as Host A or Host B) that initiates communication by sending service requests to a corresponding server process. It represents the active, requesting side in the client-server paradigm."&lt;SEP&gt;"A client process is an application-level program that requests services from a server process in a client-server communication model."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d8da4662c1f51095b9efcbe2f2cbe556&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ad6543c9c55fc16571200afb61505ac0</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;SERVER PROCESS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A Server Process is an application-layer software entity residing on a host (e.g., Host C) that listens for and responds to incoming requests from client processes. Multiple server processes can run concurrently on a single host, serving many clients simultaneously."&lt;SEP&gt;"A server process is an application-level program that provides services to client processes upon request in a client-server architecture."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d8da4662c1f51095b9efcbe2f2cbe556&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ad6543c9c55fc16571200afb61505ac0</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;IP PROTOCOL&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The IP (Internet Protocol) is the central protocol in the TCP/IP suite, forming the narrow waist of the sandglass architecture. It enables universal internetworking by allowing diverse transport-layer protocols to operate above it and supporting operation over heterogeneous underlying network technologies below it."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-ad6543c9c55fc16571200afb61505ac0</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 5}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 28}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;WAN&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Wide Area Network (WAN) is a type of computer network that spans a large geographical area, used to connect smaller networks like LANs or MANs."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d8da4662c1f51095b9efcbe2f2cbe556</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;MAN&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Metropolitan Area Network (MAN) is a computer network that interconnects users within a geographic area larger than a LAN but smaller than a WAN, typically covering a city."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d8da4662c1f51095b9efcbe2f2cbe556</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;LAN&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Local Area Network (LAN) is a computer network that connects devices within a limited area such as a home, school, or office building."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d8da4662c1f51095b9efcbe2f2cbe556</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;PAN&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Personal Area Network (PAN) is a computer network used for data transmission among devices such as computers, smartphones, and tablets within an individual’s workspace."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d8da4662c1f51095b9efcbe2f2cbe556</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;NETWORK LAYER&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Also called the internet layer, this layer handles logical addressing and routing of data packets across networks, primarily using the IP protocol."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d8da4662c1f51095b9efcbe2f2cbe556</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 8}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 38}, {"level": 2, "cluster": 57}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;DATA LINK LAYER&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"This layer provides node-to-node data transfer and handles error detection and correction from the physical layer."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d8da4662c1f51095b9efcbe2f2cbe556</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;PHYSICAL LAYER&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The lowest layer in the network model, dealing with the physical connection between devices and transmission of raw bit streams over a medium."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d8da4662c1f51095b9efcbe2f2cbe556</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;THREE-TIER ISP STRUCTURE&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The hierarchical structure of Internet Service Providers (ISPs) consisting of tier-1, tier-2, and tier-3 providers that collectively form the backbone and access infrastructure of the Internet."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d8da4662c1f51095b9efcbe2f2cbe556</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 5}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 25}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;CLIENT-SERVER MODEL&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CONCEPT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The client-server model is a network architecture where clients request services and servers provide them; it is contrasted with P2P communication in the text."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-b44f82ed51f5dd17e63648288cc859c4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;PEER-TO-PEER (P2P) COMMUNICATION&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CONCEPT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Peer-to-peer communication is a decentralized networking model where each node acts as both client and server; discussed in comparison to client-server in question 1-13."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-b44f82ed51f5dd17e63648288cc859c4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;NETWORK DELAY&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CONCEPT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Network delay (or latency) is the time taken for data to traverse the network; analyzed in relation to utilization and transmission/propagation components."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-b44f82ed51f5dd17e63648288cc859c4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;TRANSMISSION DELAY&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CONCEPT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Transmission delay is the time required to push all packet bits into the link, dependent on packet size and data rate; calculated in questions 1-17 and 1-29."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-b44f82ed51f5dd17e63648288cc859c4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;PROPAGATION DELAY&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CONCEPT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Propagation delay is the time for a signal to travel across a physical medium, determined by distance and propagation speed; featured in multiple calculation problems."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-b44f82ed51f5dd17e63648288cc859c4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;PROTOCOL STACK&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CONCEPT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The protocol stack refers to the layered architecture of network protocols (e.g., five-layer model); described in question 1-24 and referenced in 1-26."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-b44f82ed51f5dd17e63648288cc859c4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;TCP HEADER&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CONCEPT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A 20-byte header added by the Transmission Control Protocol at the transport layer to encapsulate application data."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-b44f82ed51f5dd17e63648288cc859c4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;IP HEADER&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CONCEPT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A 20-byte header added by the Internet Protocol at the network layer during data encapsulation."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-b44f82ed51f5dd17e63648288cc859c4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;ETHERNET FRAME HEADER AND TRAILER&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CONCEPT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"An 18-byte overhead (header + trailer) added at the data link layer when transmitting over Ethernet."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-b44f82ed51f5dd17e63648288cc859c4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;DATA TRANSMISSION EFFICIENCY&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CONCEPT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Defined as the ratio of application-layer data to total transmitted bits, including all headers and trailers; computed in question 1-19."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-b44f82ed51f5dd17e63648288cc859c4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;ROUND-TRIP TIME (RTT)&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CONCEPT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"RTT is the time for a signal to go from sender to receiver and back; used in TCP connection setup and file transfer timing models in question 1-28."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-b44f82ed51f5dd17e63648288cc859c4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;CONGESTION CONTROL&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CONCEPT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A TCP mechanism referenced in question 1-28(4), involving exponential increase in segment transmission per RTT during slow start."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-b44f82ed51f5dd17e63648288cc859c4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;SERVICE ACCESS POINT (SAP)&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CONCEPT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A conceptual point where one layer provides services to the layer above it, as defined in networking terminology in question 1-26."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-b44f82ed51f5dd17e63648288cc859c4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;PROTOCOL DATA UNIT (PDU)&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CONCEPT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The unit of data specified in a protocol layer, including headers and payload, as explained in question 1-26."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-b44f82ed51f5dd17e63648288cc859c4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;EVERYTHING OVER IP&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CONCEPT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A design principle meaning all types of communication (voice, video, etc.) can run over the Internet Protocol."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-b44f82ed51f5dd17e63648288cc859c4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;IP OVER EVERYTHING&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CONCEPT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A complementary principle indicating that IP can operate over any underlying network technology (Ethernet, ATM, etc.)."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-b44f82ed51f5dd17e63648288cc859c4</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;主机 B&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"主机 B is a destination computing device that receives data or files from 主机 A through various network configurations involving switches and links."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-c24d88fe5e8a41451cb6e5c8bf5bb54c</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 10}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 44}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;节点交换机&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"节点交换机 refers to intermediate network switching devices that forward data between 主机 A and 主机 B, either storing-and-forwarding entire messages or processing packets in real time."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-c24d88fe5e8a41451cb6e5c8bf5bb54c</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 10}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 44}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;点对点链路&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"GEO"</data>
  <data key="d1">"点对点链路 describes a physical communication line of specified length (e.g., 20000 km) connecting two endpoints, used for transmitting data with defined propagation and transmission characteristics."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-c24d88fe5e8a41451cb6e5c8bf5bb54c</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 10}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 46}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;RTT 测量&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"RTT 测量 refers to the conceptual discussion of Round-Trip Time as a key performance metric in networking, even when data transmission is unidirectional."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-c24d88fe5e8a41451cb6e5c8bf5bb54c</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 10}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 46}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;报文交换实验&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"报文交换实验 is the scenario in problem 1-34 where an entire 10^7-bit message is sent without segmentation through two node switches across three links."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-c24d88fe5e8a41451cb6e5c8bf5bb54c</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 10}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 44}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;分组交换实验&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"分组交换实验 is the scenario in problem 1-34 where a large message is divided into 1000 equal-sized packets and transmitted using store-and-forward or cut-through switching."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-c24d88fe5e8a41451cb6e5c8bf5bb54c</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 10}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 44}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;吞吐量计算事件&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"吞吐量计算事件 refers to the analysis in problem 1-36 where a 10 MB file is sent over a path with three links of different bandwidths to determine end-to-end throughput and transfer time."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-c24d88fe5e8a41451cb6e5c8bf5bb54c</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 10}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 47}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;20000 KM 链路&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"GEO"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A point-to-point communication link spanning 20,000 kilometers, used in problems 1-30 and 1-31 to analyze transmission and propagation delays."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-c24d88fe5e8a41451cb6e5c8bf5bb54c</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 10}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 43}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;5000 KM 链路&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"GEO"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A 5,000-kilometer-long network link between 主机 A and 主机 B described in problem 1-35, with a propagation speed of 2.5 × 10⁸ m/s and bandwidth of 1 Mbit/s."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-c24d88fe5e8a41451cb6e5c8bf5bb54c</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 10}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 45}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;三段链路路径&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"GEO"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A multi-hop network path consisting of three sequential links with data rates of 2 Mbit/s, 1 Mbit/s, and 500 kbit/s respectively, connecting 主机 A to 主机 B in problem 1-36."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-c24d88fe5e8a41451cb6e5c8bf5bb54c</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 10}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 44}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;1 KBIT/S 链路&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"GEO"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A low-speed communication channel operating at 1 kbit/s, referenced in problem 1-30 for delay comparison and bit visualization."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-c24d88fe5e8a41451cb6e5c8bf5bb54c</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;1 MBIT/S 链路&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"GEO"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A medium-speed link with 1 Mbit/s capacity, used in problems 1-31 and 1-35 for analyzing transmission dynamics and bit density."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-c24d88fe5e8a41451cb6e5c8bf5bb54c</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;1 GBIT/S 链路&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"GEO"</data>
  <data key="d1">"An ultra-high-speed data link operating at 1 Gbit/s, discussed in problem 1-32 to compute spatial and temporal bit width."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-c24d88fe5e8a41451cb6e5c8bf5bb54c</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;2 MBIT/S 链路&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"GEO"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A 2 Mbit/s network segment used in problem 1-34 for both message and packet switching delay calculations."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-c24d88fe5e8a41451cb6e5c8bf5bb54c</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;500 KBIT/S 链路&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"GEO"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The bottleneck link in problem 1-36 with a rate of 500 kbit/s, determining the end-to-end throughput of the path."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-c24d88fe5e8a41451cb6e5c8bf5bb54c</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 10}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 47}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;传播时延分析事件&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The conceptual exercise in early questions comparing propagation delay to transmission delay for 100-byte and 512-byte packets."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-c24d88fe5e8a41451cb6e5c8bf5bb54c</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;比特可视化事件&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"The illustrative scenario in problem 1-30 where bits are imagined as visible on a 20,000 km link at two time instants: immediately after sending 100 bits and 0.05 seconds later."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-c24d88fe5e8a41451cb6e5c8bf5bb54c</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 10}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 43}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;带宽-传播综合分析事件&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Problem 1-35’s examination of maximum bits on a link, bit width in meters, and required transmission rate to match link length with one-bit width."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-c24d88fe5e8a41451cb6e5c8bf5bb54c</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 10}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 45}]</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;INTERNET&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">Based on the provided descriptions, a comprehensive summary of **INTERNET** is as follows:

**The Internet (capitalized "I")** refers to the specific, global, open computer network that is the largest and most important interconnected network in the world. It evolved from the U.S. ARPANET, with its formal birth marked by the adoption of the TCP/IP protocol suite as the standard on ARPANET in 1983. The Internet operates fundamentally on the TCP/IP protocol suite, particularly leveraging the Internet Protocol (IP) at its core layer, which enables communication across diverse underlying network types ("IP over everything") and supports a vast range of transport and application layer protocols ("everything over IP"). This embodies a key design philosophy where the network core remains simple,</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-076a966631561cc724a628cc319a15f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca0c22897ac4785fecc70607badaf9cb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e05b6bd70dc7a04d2758501d65696eff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8da4662c1f51095b9efcbe2f2cbe556&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b07dbbacc9a0b17e8854aaf7b97228e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd06b8e7be2529cda8d9408c8ba624d5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e7c449009dbc73e669666df32bd7aeb1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4b9e864a515f0d66754933635238c92d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ad6543c9c55fc16571200afb61505ac0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-982f03b0a500a9e6bd7272aaa9e050d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2ab20554e89073ba513e052dbf5667c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c827098a559a559af3c8df103949b45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30cc6309bbf7dc040e88d043d89a8722</data>
  <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 5}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 25}]</data>
</node>
