<edge source="&quot;互联网&quot;" target="&quot;智能手机&quot;">
  <data key="d4">8.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"The textbook notes that smartphones have become the main devices connecting to the Internet, illustrating a major evolution in Internet usage."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-243d8875e135092451e82e6ee7250806</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;互联网&quot;" target="&quot;美国&quot;">
  <data key="d4">9.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"The United States is the birthplace of the internet, establishing its foundational origin and historical development."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-561c228fa7944971c082eed5e584dc98</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;互联网&quot;" target="&quot;互联网瘫痪事件&quot;">
  <data key="d4">10.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"The hypothetical 'internet outage event' directly stems from the failure of internet services, demonstrating the critical role and societal reliance on the internet as infrastructure."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-561c228fa7944971c082eed5e584dc98</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;互联网&quot;" target="&quot;主机 A&quot;">
  <data key="d4">9.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Host A’s throughput is directly affected by congestion anywhere in the internet, illustrating dependency on global network conditions beyond local control."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-0174bb3213f0a9872650fbd9b001dae3</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;互联网&quot;" target="&quot;ISP&quot;">
  <data key="d4">7.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"The ISP provides access to the internet but does not control conditions beyond its own infrastructure, highlighting a boundary of responsibility within the larger internet."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-0174bb3213f0a9872650fbd9b001dae3</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;ETHERNET&quot;" target="&quot;THROUGHPUT&quot;">
  <data key="d4">7.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"A 1 Gbit/s Ethernet connection sets an upper bound on throughput for devices using that link."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-a2ab20554e89073ba513e052dbf5667c</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;IP&quot;" target="&quot;TCP/IP&quot;">
  <data key="d4">10.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"IP is the foundational network-layer protocol of the TCP/IP suite, enabling internetwork communication through packet routing."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-4ffae866e92afc591d3a1c26480aeeb3</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;IP&quot;" target="&quot;IETF&quot;">
  <data key="d4">10.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"The IETF is responsible for the standardization and evolution of the Internet Protocol (IP), including IPv4 and IPv6."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-4ffae866e92afc591d3a1c26480aeeb3</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;IP&quot;" target="&quot;NETWORK LAYER&quot;">
  <data key="d4">10.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"IP is the primary protocol of the network layer, responsible for addressing and routing packets."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-d8da4662c1f51095b9efcbe2f2cbe556</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;UDP&quot;" target="&quot;TCP/IP&quot;">
  <data key="d4">10.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"UDP is a core transport protocol in the TCP/IP model, offering lightweight, best-effort data transmission."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-4ffae866e92afc591d3a1c26480aeeb3</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;UDP&quot;" target="&quot;IETF&quot;">
  <data key="d4">9.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"The IETF standardizes the User Datagram Protocol (UDP) as part of the core internet protocol specifications."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-4ffae866e92afc591d3a1c26480aeeb3</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;UDP&quot;" target="&quot;TRANSPORT LAYER&quot;">
  <data key="d4">9.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"UDP is a transport layer protocol used for lightweight, connectionless communication."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-d8da4662c1f51095b9efcbe2f2cbe556</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;TCP&quot;" target="&quot;INTERNET&quot;">
  <data key="d4">6.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"TCP is a fundamental protocol used in Internet communications, referenced in the text as an example of widely accepted English acronyms in Chinese technical writing related to the Internet."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-4b9e864a515f0d66754933635238c92d</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;TCP&quot;" target="&quot;TCP/IP&quot;">
  <data key="d4">10.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"TCP is a fundamental transport protocol within the TCP/IP architecture, providing reliable data delivery services."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-4ffae866e92afc591d3a1c26480aeeb3</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;TCP&quot;" target="&quot;IETF&quot;">
  <data key="d4">9.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"The IETF developed and maintains the official standards for the Transmission Control Protocol (TCP)."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-4ffae866e92afc591d3a1c26480aeeb3</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;TCP&quot;" target="&quot;TRANSPORT LAYER&quot;">
  <data key="d4">10.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"TCP is one of the two main protocols of the transport layer, providing reliable data transmission."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-d8da4662c1f51095b9efcbe2f2cbe556</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;DNS&quot;" target="&quot;TCP/IP&quot;">
  <data key="d4">9.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"DNS operates as an application-layer protocol within the TCP/IP protocol suite, relying on its transport and network layers for functionality."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-4ffae866e92afc591d3a1c26480aeeb3</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;DNS&quot;" target="&quot;IETF&quot;">
  <data key="d4">8.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"The IETF standardizes the DNS protocol specifications, ensuring interoperability across the internet."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-4ffae866e92afc591d3a1c26480aeeb3</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;HTTP&quot;" target="&quot;TCP/IP&quot;">
  <data key="d4">9.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"HTTP is an application-layer protocol designed to function over the TCP/IP stack, specifically using TCP for reliable data delivery."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-4ffae866e92afc591d3a1c26480aeeb3</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;HTTP&quot;" target="&quot;IETF&quot;">
  <data key="d4">8.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"The IETF, along with W3C, plays a key role in standardizing HTTP protocols used in web communications."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-4ffae866e92afc591d3a1c26480aeeb3</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;SMTP&quot;" target="&quot;TCP/IP&quot;">
  <data key="d4">9.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"SMTP is an application-layer protocol that uses TCP/IP for message transmission across networks."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-4ffae866e92afc591d3a1c26480aeeb3</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;SMTP&quot;" target="&quot;IETF&quot;">
  <data key="d4">8.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"The IETF maintains and publishes the official standards for SMTP as part of internet messaging infrastructure."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-4ffae866e92afc591d3a1c26480aeeb3</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;P2P&quot;" target="&quot;CLIENT PROCESS&quot;">
  <data key="d4">7.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Unlike traditional client-server models, P2P allows each node to act as both client and server, representing an alternative communication paradigm."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-d8da4662c1f51095b9efcbe2f2cbe556</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;全国科学技术名词审定委员会&quot;" target="&quot;INTERNET&quot;">
  <data key="d4">7.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"The committee officially recommended '因特网' as the standardized Chinese translation for 'Internet', influencing terminology usage in academic and technical contexts in China."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-4b9e864a515f0d66754933635238c92d</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;中国&quot;" target="&quot;INTERNET&quot;">
  <data key="d4">9.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"In China, the term '互联网' has become the de facto standard for referring to the Internet across media, government, and public usage, shaping how the population interacts with and conceptualizes the network."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-4b9e864a515f0d66754933635238c92d</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;中国&quot;" target="&quot;爱特信公司&quot;">
  <data key="d4">8.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Aitexin Company was established in China as the country’s first venture capital-funded internet company."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-3cf7f9315ae398e3c73bd500746d26d8</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;中国&quot;" target="&quot;网易公司&quot;">
  <data key="d4">8.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"NetEase was founded in China and became a major domestic internet player with locally tailored services."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-3cf7f9315ae398e3c73bd500746d26d8</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;中国&quot;" target="&quot;新浪网站&quot;">
  <data key="d4">8.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Sina.com was founded in China and grew to become the world’s largest Chinese-language portal, serving primarily Chinese-speaking users."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-3cf7f9315ae398e3c73bd500746d26d8</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;中国&quot;" target="&quot;腾讯公司&quot;">
  <data key="d4">9.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Tencent was founded in China and developed products like QQ and WeChat that became essential to Chinese internet users."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-3cf7f9315ae398e3c73bd500746d26d8</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;中国&quot;" target="&quot;百度网站&quot;">
  <data key="d4">9.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Baidu operates in China and became the dominant search engine after Google’s departure, catering specifically to the Chinese market."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-3cf7f9315ae398e3c73bd500746d26d8</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;中国&quot;" target="&quot;阿里巴巴网站&quot;">
  <data key="d4">8.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Alibaba.com was founded in China to serve as a B2B e-commerce platform primarily for Chinese businesses."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-3cf7f9315ae398e3c73bd500746d26d8</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;三网融合&quot;" target="&quot;INTERNET&quot;">
  <data key="d4">8.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"The Internet is central to the concept of '三网融合', as it increasingly incorporates functionalities of telecommunications and cable TV networks, driving the convergence of the three major network types."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-4b9e864a515f0d66754933635238c92d</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;三网融合&quot;" target="&quot;TELECOMMUNICATIONS NETWORK&quot;">
  <data key="d4">7.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"The telecommunications network is one of the three networks targeted for integration under the '三网融合' initiative, aiming to unify service delivery across network types."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-4b9e864a515f0d66754933635238c92d</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;三网融合&quot;" target="&quot;CABLE TELEVISION NETWORK&quot;">
  <data key="d4">7.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"The cable television network is another component of the '三网融合' vision, intended to merge with computer and telecom networks into a single multifunctional infrastructure."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-4b9e864a515f0d66754933635238c92d</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;20世纪90年代以后互联网的飞速发展&quot;" target="&quot;INTERNET&quot;">
  <data key="d4">10.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"This event marks the transformation of the Internet into a global commercial network, establishing its role as the most significant computer network in human history since the invention of printing."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-4b9e864a515f0d66754933635238c92d</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;COMPUTER NETWORK&quot;" target="&quot;INTERNET&quot;">
  <data key="d4">18.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Multiple computer networks are interconnected via routers to form the internet, making the computer network a fundamental constituent of the internet."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Internet is a specific instance and global extension of computer networks; computer networks form the foundational technology upon which the Internet is built."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-4b9e864a515f0d66754933635238c92d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd06b8e7be2529cda8d9408c8ba624d5</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;COMPUTER NETWORK&quot;" target="&quot;INFORMATION AGE&quot;">
  <data key="d4">10.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Computer networks are the core enabler of the Information Age, driving digitization, informatization, and societal transformation through rapid data exchange."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-4b9e864a515f0d66754933635238c92d</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;COMPUTER NETWORK&quot;" target="&quot;HOST&quot;">
  <data key="d4">8.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Hosts are computers connected to a computer network and participate in communication across the network; they are distinct from internal networking devices like routers."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-bd06b8e7be2529cda8d9408c8ba624d5</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;COMPUTER NETWORK&quot;" target="&quot;HUB&quot;">
  <data key="d4">8.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Hubs are used as central connection points within a simple computer network, as illustrated in Figure 1-1(a), where three computers connect to a hub via links."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-bd06b8e7be2529cda8d9408c8ba624d5</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;COMPUTER NETWORK&quot;" target="&quot;SWITCH&quot;">
  <data key="d4">8.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Switches function as internal nodes in a computer network, facilitating communication between connected devices by directing data traffic efficiently."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-bd06b8e7be2529cda8d9408c8ba624d5</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;COMPUTER NETWORK&quot;" target="&quot;ROUTER&quot;">
  <data key="d4">9.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Routers interconnect multiple computer networks to form larger internetworks, acting as boundary devices between distinct networks."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-bd06b8e7be2529cda8d9408c8ba624d5</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;ONLINE BANKING&quot;" target="&quot;INTERNET&quot;">
  <data key="d4">8.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"The Internet provides the platform and connectivity required for online banking services, making remote financial operations possible and widespread."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-4b9e864a515f0d66754933635238c92d</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;E-COMMERCE&quot;" target="&quot;INTERNET&quot;">
  <data key="d4">9.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"E-commerce relies entirely on the Internet’s infrastructure for transaction processing, product display, and customer interaction, representing a key application of its commercial evolution."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-4b9e864a515f0d66754933635238c92d</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;SOCIAL COMMUNICATION VIA INTERNET&quot;" target="&quot;INTERNET&quot;">
  <data key="d4">9.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"The Internet enables modern social communication through email, chat, and media sharing, fundamentally altering how people interact and maintain relationships."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-4b9e864a515f0d66754933635238c92d</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;银行&quot;" target="&quot;CNPAC&quot;">
  <data key="d4">5.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Banking institutions developed dedicated networks in the late 1980s, contemporaneously with the launch of CNPAC, indicating sector-wide adoption of networking."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-779592fbef47ea27eab563c9f2b5fb8a</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;ROUTER&quot;" target="&quot;INTERNET&quot;">
  <data key="d4">38.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Routers are essential for constructing the internet, as they interconnect separate computer networks to create a larger internetwork."&lt;SEP&gt;"Routers form the core infrastructure of the internet, interconnecting multiple networks to enable global data transmission."&lt;SEP&gt;"Routers interconnect the heterogeneous networks that constitute the Internet, enabling global packet delivery."&lt;SEP&gt;"Routers operate in the core part of the Internet and enable packet switching via store-and-forward mechanisms."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-d8da4662c1f51095b9efcbe2f2cbe556&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b07dbbacc9a0b17e8854aaf7b97228e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c827098a559a559af3c8df103949b45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd06b8e7be2529cda8d9408c8ba624d5</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;ROUTER&quot;" target="&quot;PACKET&quot;">
  <data key="d4">9.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Routers receive, store, inspect the header of, and forward packets based on destination address information."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-b07dbbacc9a0b17e8854aaf7b97228e2</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;ROUTER&quot;" target="&quot;PACKET SWITCHING&quot;">
  <data key="d4">10.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Routers execute packet switching by storing and forwarding packets through the network."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-b07dbbacc9a0b17e8854aaf7b97228e2</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;ROUTER&quot;" target="&quot;ROUTING TABLE&quot;">
  <data key="d4">10.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Routers use and dynamically maintain routing tables to determine how to forward packets."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-b07dbbacc9a0b17e8854aaf7b97228e2</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;ROUTER&quot;" target="&quot;IP (INTERNET PROTOCOL)&quot;">
  <data key="d4">10.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Routers use the IP protocol to make forwarding decisions based on destination addresses in packet headers."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-1c827098a559a559af3c8df103949b45</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;ROUTER&quot;" target="&quot;HOST&quot;">
  <data key="d4">8.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Hosts communicate across the Internet by sending packets through routers, which forward them toward their destination."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-1c827098a559a559af3c8df103949b45</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;HOST&quot;" target="&quot;PACKET&quot;">
  <data key="d4">8.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Hosts generate and consume packets, sending them into the network and receiving them from the network as part of communication."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-b07dbbacc9a0b17e8854aaf7b97228e2</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;HOST&quot;" target="&quot;INTERNET&quot;">
  <data key="d4">16.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Hosts operate in the edge part of the Internet and perform information processing tasks for users and applications."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hosts reside at the edge of the internet and rely on it to exchange information with other hosts."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-d8da4662c1f51095b9efcbe2f2cbe556&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b07dbbacc9a0b17e8854aaf7b97228e2</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;HOST&quot;" target="&quot;APPLICATION PROCESS AP₁&quot;">
  <data key="d4">7.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"AP₁ operates on Host 1 as its local application process, initiating outbound communication."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-1c827098a559a559af3c8df103949b45</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;HOST&quot;" target="&quot;APPLICATION PROCESS AP₂&quot;">
  <data key="d4">7.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"AP₂ resides on Host 2 and receives incoming data from the network stack."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-1c827098a559a559af3c8df103949b45</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;ARPANET&quot;" target="&quot;INTERNET&quot;">
  <data key="d4">28.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"ARPANET is the direct predecessor of the Internet; the adoption of TCP/IP on ARPANET in 1983 is considered the birth of the Internet."&lt;SEP&gt;"ARPANET is the historical predecessor of the Internet, directly contributing to its architecture and development."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-d8da4662c1f51095b9efcbe2f2cbe556&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-076a966631561cc724a628cc319a15f3</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;ARPANET&quot;" target="&quot;ARPA&quot;">
  <data key="d4">9.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"ARPA developed and managed ARPANET, initiating the research that led to network interconnection technologies."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-076a966631561cc724a628cc319a15f3</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;ARPANET&quot;" target="&quot;UNITED STATES&quot;">
  <data key="d4">41.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"ARPANET operated within the United States, with performance metrics specifically referencing cross-country (East to West Coast) communication."&lt;SEP&gt;"ARPANET was created and operated within the United States by the U.S. Department of Defense."&lt;SEP&gt;"ARPANET was developed in the United States, establishing a clear geographic and institutional origin."&lt;SEP&gt;"ARPANET was operational within the United States, with documented performance metrics for data transmission across its East and West Coasts."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-d8da4662c1f51095b9efcbe2f2cbe556&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dbda231b683f35c9fc91e9cc64fa2b9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-076a966631561cc724a628cc319a15f3</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;ARPANET&quot;" target="&quot;PACKET SWITCHING&quot;">
  <data key="d4">10.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"ARPANET implemented packet switching technology and provided empirical validation of its performance, including low latency across long distances."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-1dbda231b683f35c9fc91e9cc64fa2b9</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;ARPANET&quot;" target="&quot;OPEN SYSTEMS INTERCONNECTION REFERENCE MODEL (OSI/RM)&quot;">
  <data key="d4">8.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"The layered approach first used in ARPANET influenced the development of structured network models like the OSI/RM, which also adopted a layered architecture to manage complexity."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-8fe573a1edca72839801eaf0704a5d62</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;ARPANET&quot;" target="&quot;主机 1&quot;">
  <data key="d4">6.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"ARPANET's protocol design principles inform the layered communication approach used between Host 1 and other networked systems like Host 2."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-a1b5e11d873f879fd9610fda65292066</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;ARPANET&quot;" target="&quot;主机 2&quot;">
  <data key="d4">6.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"ARPANET's protocol design principles inform the layered communication approach used between Host 2 and other networked systems like Host 1."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-a1b5e11d873f879fd9610fda65292066</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;NSF&quot;" target="&quot;NSFNET&quot;">
  <data key="d4">10.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"NSF created and operated NSFNET as a three-tiered national research network that became a major backbone of the Internet in the 1980s and early 1990s."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-076a966631561cc724a628cc319a15f3</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;NSFNET&quot;" target="&quot;INTERNET&quot;">
  <data key="d4">8.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"NSFNET became a primary component of the Internet’s infrastructure during its second developmental stage, facilitating expansion beyond military and research use."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-076a966631561cc724a628cc319a15f3</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;NSFNET&quot;" target="&quot;UNITED STATES&quot;">
  <data key="d4">9.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"NSFNET was developed and deployed across the United States by the National Science Foundation, connecting universities and research institutions nationwide."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-076a966631561cc724a628cc319a15f3</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;NSFNET&quot;" target="&quot;U.S. GOVERNMENT&quot;">
  <data key="d4">9.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"The U.S. Government funded and operated NSFNET as part of its early stewardship of internet infrastructure."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-7cb534a20cec43f62bad499beb758a78</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;NSFNET&quot;" target="&quot;TRANSITION FROM NSFNET TO COMMERCIAL BACKBONES&quot;">
  <data key="d4">10.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"NSFNET was phased out as part of this transition event, being replaced by commercial internet backbones."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-7cb534a20cec43f62bad499beb758a78</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;TCP/IP&quot;" target="&quot;OSI&quot;">
  <data key="d4">18.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"OSI and TCP/IP are both reference models for network communication; the text contrasts OSI's seven-layer model with TCP/IP's four-layer model, indicating a conceptual and historical relationship in networking architecture."&lt;SEP&gt;"OSI and TCP/IP are competing networking frameworks; TCP/IP achieved market dominance while OSI failed commercially despite being an official international standard, creating a historical contrast between theoretical standardization and practical adoption."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-4ffae866e92afc591d3a1c26480aeeb3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d24b4f467e6a4552877bf9e42c614b2</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;TCP/IP&quot;" target="&quot;TCP/IP FOUR-LAYER MODEL&quot;">
  <data key="d4">10.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"TCP/IP is the foundational protocol suite that implements the four-layer architecture described in the text."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-e05b6bd70dc7a04d2758501d65696eff</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;TCP/IP&quot;" target="&quot;IETF&quot;">
  <data key="d4">18.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"The IETF is the primary organization responsible for developing and maintaining the TCP/IP protocol standards."&lt;SEP&gt;"The IETF maintains and standardizes the TCP/IP protocol suite, though it does not define link-layer protocols."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-4ffae866e92afc591d3a1c26480aeeb3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e05b6bd70dc7a04d2758501d65696eff</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;TCP/IP&quot;" target="&quot;INTERNET&quot;">
  <data key="d4">30.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"TCP/IP is the fundamental protocol suite that enables communication over the Internet."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Internet uses TCP/IP as its standard communication protocol suite, making this a foundational technical relationship."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-d8da4662c1f51095b9efcbe2f2cbe556&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e05b6bd70dc7a04d2758501d65696eff</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;TCP/IP&quot;" target="&quot;蓝军 1&quot;">
  <data key="d4">6.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"The scenario involving 蓝军 1 illustrates a fundamental problem in communication protocols—similar to challenges addressed (but not fully solvable) in real-world systems like TCP/IP, which relies on acknowledgments for reliable transmission."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-77d64847854ffc2a5d57b6da6e7bc7d1</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;TCP/IP&quot;" target="&quot;蓝军 2&quot;">
  <data key="d4">6.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Similar to 蓝军 1, 蓝军 2’s situation reflects the limitations of acknowledgment-based protocols, a concept relevant to the design and critique of TCP/IP’s reliability mechanisms."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-77d64847854ffc2a5d57b6da6e7bc7d1</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;ISP&quot;" target="&quot;GLOBAL INTERNET&quot;">
  <data key="d4">10.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"The modern Global Internet is collectively owned and operated by numerous ISPs worldwide, forming a 'network of networks.'"</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-7cb534a20cec43f62bad499beb758a78</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;ISP&quot;" target="&quot;CHINA TELECOM&quot;">
  <data key="d4">8.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"China Telecom is a specific example of a national ISP that provides internet services within the broader ISP framework."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-7cb534a20cec43f62bad499beb758a78</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;ISP&quot;" target="&quot;CHINA UNICOM&quot;">
  <data key="d4">8.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"China Unicom operates as a major ISP in China, fitting into the global ISP hierarchy described in the text."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-7cb534a20cec43f62bad499beb758a78</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;ISP&quot;" target="&quot;CHINA MOBILE&quot;">
  <data key="d4">8.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"China Mobile functions as a key ISP in China, exemplifying the commercial internet service provider model."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-7cb534a20cec43f62bad499beb758a78</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;ISP&quot;" target="&quot;IXP&quot;">
  <data key="d4">9.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"IXPs enable direct interconnection between ISPs (especially regional ones), optimizing data exchange and reducing dependency on backbone ISPs."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-7cb534a20cec43f62bad499beb758a78</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;ISP&quot;" target="&quot;HOST A&quot;">
  <data key="d4">15.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Host A accesses the internet through a hierarchy of ISPs (local, regional, possibly backbone), illustrating how end users rely on ISP infrastructure."&lt;SEP&gt;"The ISP provides Host A with broadband Internet access at a contracted rate of 100 Mbit/s."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-7cb534a20cec43f62bad499beb758a78&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2ab20554e89073ba513e052dbf5667c</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;ISP&quot;" target="&quot;HOST B&quot;">
  <data key="d4">7.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Host B similarly connects to the internet via the layered ISP structure, enabling communication with Host A."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-7cb534a20cec43f62bad499beb758a78</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;ISP&quot;" target="&quot;IP ADDRESS MANAGEMENT AUTHORITY&quot;">
  <data key="d4">9.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"The IP address management authority allocates large blocks of IP addresses to qualified ISPs, enabling them to distribute addresses to their customers."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-7cb534a20cec43f62bad499beb758a78</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;ISP&quot;" target="&quot;INTERNET&quot;">
  <data key="d4">8.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"ISPs provide essential connectivity to the Internet for end systems and may also operate their own hosts, acting as both service providers and participants in the Internet ecosystem."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-30cc6309bbf7dc040e88d043d89a8722</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;ISP&quot;" target="&quot;主机 A&quot;">
  <data key="d4">8.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"主机 A pays the ISP for a broadband connection with a specified rate, but the ISP only guarantees performance up to its own router, not beyond into the wider internet."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-0174bb3213f0a9872650fbd9b001dae3</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;IXP&quot;" target="&quot;HOST A&quot;">
  <data key="d4">5.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"By facilitating direct ISP interconnection, IXPs can optimize the communication path between Host A and other hosts like Host B."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-7cb534a20cec43f62bad499beb758a78</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;IXP&quot;" target="&quot;HOST B&quot;">
  <data key="d4">5.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"IXPs improve the efficiency of data exchange involving Host B by allowing regional ISPs to peer directly rather than route through backbone providers."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-7cb534a20cec43f62bad499beb758a78</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;IXP&quot;" target="&quot;W-PCH&quot;">
  <data key="d4">8.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"W-PCH provided statistical data on the global number and geographic distribution of IXPs in August 2020, establishing a clear reporting relationship."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-c4a2f7654873f1e1c6f37434762f36ae</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;IXP&quot;" target="&quot;SHANGHAI&quot;">
  <data key="d4">8.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Shanghai hosts 5 IXPs, indicating a strong geographic association between the city and these internet exchange organizations."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-c4a2f7654873f1e1c6f37434762f36ae</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;IXP&quot;" target="&quot;GUANGZHOU&quot;">
  <data key="d4">8.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Guangzhou hosts 4 IXPs, indicating a strong geographic association between the city and these internet exchange organizations."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-c4a2f7654873f1e1c6f37434762f36ae</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;IXP&quot;" target="&quot;HONG KONG&quot;">
  <data key="d4">8.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Hong Kong hosts 9 IXPs, indicating a strong geographic association between the region and these internet exchange organizations."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-c4a2f7654873f1e1c6f37434762f36ae</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;IXP&quot;" target="&quot;TAIWAN&quot;">
  <data key="d4">8.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Taiwan hosts 8 IXPs, indicating a strong geographic association between the region and these internet exchange organizations."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-c4a2f7654873f1e1c6f37434762f36ae</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;IXP&quot;" target="&quot;LOCAL ISP&quot;">
  <data key="d4">7.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Local ISPs connect to IXPs to exchange traffic, but must pay fees when using the IXP to forward packets to higher-tier networks, indicating a conditional commercial and operational relationship."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-c4a2f7654873f1e1c6f37434762f36ae</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;IXP&quot;" target="&quot;REGIONAL ISP&quot;">
  <data key="d4">9.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Regional ISPs peer with each other at IXPs to exchange packets directly without charge, improving efficiency and reducing latency, showing a strong operational and economic relationship."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-c4a2f7654873f1e1c6f37434762f36ae</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;IXP&quot;" target="&quot;CONTENT PROVIDER&quot;">
  <data key="d4">8.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Content Providers connect their private networks to IXPs to deliver video content more efficiently to users across multiple ISPs, establishing a strategic interconnection relationship."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-c4a2f7654873f1e1c6f37434762f36ae</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;HOST A&quot;" target="&quot;HOST B&quot;">
  <data key="d4">15.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Host A and Host B communicate across the internet through various ISP layers, with IXPs potentially enabling more direct paths between them."&lt;SEP&gt;"Host A communicates with Host B in a client-server relationship: A requests services and B provides them, as defined in Figure 1-5."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-7cb534a20cec43f62bad499beb758a78&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7076395e2a9a9ea6cdf6d289a0503201</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;HOST A&quot;" target="&quot;LOCAL ISP&quot;">
  <data key="d4">8.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Host A connects to the internet through a local ISP, which serves as its direct point of access to the global network."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-7cb534a20cec43f62bad499beb758a78</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;HOST A&quot;" target="&quot;FIGURE 1-3&quot;">
  <data key="d4">6.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Figure 1-3 visually depicts the communication path of Host A through multiple ISP layers to reach Host B."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-7cb534a20cec43f62bad499beb758a78</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;HOST A&quot;" target="&quot;CUSTOMER-SERVER COMMUNICATION&quot;">
  <data key="d4">8.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Host A is a key participant in the Customer-Server Communication event, acting as the initiating client."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-7076395e2a9a9ea6cdf6d289a0503201</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;HOST A&quot;" target="&quot;SERVER B&quot;">
  <data key="d4">8.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Host A receives data from Server B over the Internet, with their communication throughput limited by the slower link or shared server capacity."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-a2ab20554e89073ba513e052dbf5667c</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;HOST A&quot;" target="&quot;INTERNET&quot;">
  <data key="d4">7.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Host A accesses services via the Internet, with its performance dependent on Internet conditions and link capacity."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-a2ab20554e89073ba513e052dbf5667c</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;HOST A&quot;" target="&quot;HOST C&quot;">
  <data key="d4">8.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Host A communicates with Host C through its client process (Client 1) interacting with Server 1 on Host C, demonstrating a direct client-server relationship across networked hosts."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-ad6543c9c55fc16571200afb61505ac0</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;HOST B&quot;" target="&quot;LOCAL ISP&quot;">
  <data key="d4">8.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Host B similarly accesses the internet via a local ISP, completing the end-to-end communication path."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-7cb534a20cec43f62bad499beb758a78</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;HOST B&quot;" target="&quot;FIGURE 1-3&quot;">
  <data key="d4">6.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Figure 1-3 includes Host B as the destination in the illustrated multi-tier ISP communication scenario."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-7cb534a20cec43f62bad499beb758a78</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;HOST B&quot;" target="&quot;CUSTOMER-SERVER COMMUNICATION&quot;">
  <data key="d4">8.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Host B plays the server role in the Customer-Server Communication event, responding to client requests."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-7076395e2a9a9ea6cdf6d289a0503201</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;HOST B&quot;" target="&quot;HOST C&quot;">
  <data key="d4">8.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Host B communicates with Host C via its client process (Client 2) connecting to Server 2 on Host C, illustrating another instance of distributed client-server communication."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-ad6543c9c55fc16571200afb61505ac0</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;GLOBAL INTERNET&quot;" target="&quot;BACKBONE ISP&quot;">
  <data key="d4">9.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Backbone ISPs form the core infrastructure of the Global Internet, enabling intercontinental and nationwide connectivity."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-7cb534a20cec43f62bad499beb758a78</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;BACKBONE ISP&quot;" target="&quot;REGIONAL ISP&quot;">
  <data key="d4">9.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Regional ISPs connect to one or more backbone ISPs to gain access to the broader internet, forming a hierarchical dependency in the ISP structure."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-7cb534a20cec43f62bad499beb758a78</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;REGIONAL ISP&quot;" target="&quot;LOCAL ISP&quot;">
  <data key="d4">8.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Local ISPs typically connect to regional ISPs (or sometimes directly to backbone ISPs) to provide end users with internet access, establishing a service chain."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-7cb534a20cec43f62bad499beb758a78</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;REGIONAL ISP&quot;" target="&quot;INTERNET EXCHANGE POINT (IXP)&quot;">
  <data key="d4">9.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Regional ISPs use IXPs to peer directly with each other, bypassing backbone ISPs for more efficient traffic exchange, as illustrated in Figure 1-3."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-7cb534a20cec43f62bad499beb758a78</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;REGIONAL ISP&quot;" target="&quot;CONTENT PROVIDER&quot;">
  <data key="d4">8.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Content Providers interconnect with Regional ISPs to optimize delivery of high-bandwidth video content to end-users, forming a critical content-delivery partnership."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-c4a2f7654873f1e1c6f37434762f36ae</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;LOCAL ISP&quot;" target="&quot;UNIVERSITY&quot;">
  <data key="d4">7.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"A university can function as a local ISP by operating its own network and providing internet services to its members, fitting within the local ISP category."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-7cb534a20cec43f62bad499beb758a78</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;LOCAL ISP&quot;" target="&quot;ENTERPRISE&quot;">
  <data key="d4">7.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"An enterprise may act as a local ISP when it provides internet connectivity to its employees through its internal network infrastructure."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-7cb534a20cec43f62bad499beb758a78</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;LOCAL ISP&quot;" target="&quot;CONTENT PROVIDER&quot;">
  <data key="d4">7.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Content Providers also connect to Local ISPs or their upstream networks to ensure last-mile delivery of video content, creating an indirect but essential service relationship."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-c4a2f7654873f1e1c6f37434762f36ae</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;LOCAL ISP&quot;" target="&quot;ACCESS NETWORK (AN)&quot;">
  <data key="d4">10.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Local ISPs own and manage access networks, which directly connect users to the internet, making this a direct operational relationship."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-982f03b0a500a9e6bd7272aaa9e050d8</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;LOCAL ISP&quot;" target="&quot;INTERNET&quot;">
  <data key="d4">9.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Local ISPs provide the critical link that connects end users to the internet, acting as gateways to the global network."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-982f03b0a500a9e6bd7272aaa9e050d8</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;ENTERPRISE&quot;" target="&quot;INTERNET&quot;">
  <data key="d4">6.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Enterprises utilize the Internet by connecting their end systems to communicate and share resources, forming part of the Internet's user base."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-30cc6309bbf7dc040e88d043d89a8722</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;DE-CIX&quot;" target="&quot;FRANKFURT&quot;">
  <data key="d4">10.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"DE-CIX is physically located in Frankfurt, Germany, making Frankfurt its host city."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-c4a2f7654873f1e1c6f37434762f36ae</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;DE-CIX&quot;" target="&quot;GERMANY&quot;">
  <data key="d4">9.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"DE-CIX operates in Germany, specifically in Frankfurt, linking it directly to the country as its national location."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-c4a2f7654873f1e1c6f37434762f36ae</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;CERN&quot;" target="&quot;WORLD WIDE WEB (WWW)&quot;">
  <data key="d4">10.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"CERN developed the World Wide Web, directly creating this transformative event that reshaped internet usage."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-c4a2f7654873f1e1c6f37434762f36ae</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;INTERNET SOCIETY (ISOC)&quot;" target="&quot;FORMATION OF ISOC&quot;">
  <data key="d4">10.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"The Formation of ISOC in 1992 established the Internet Society as a formal international body, making this event foundational to ISOC's existence."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-c4a2f7654873f1e1c6f37434762f36ae</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;INTERNET SOCIETY (ISOC)&quot;" target="&quot;INTERNET ARCHITECTURE BOARD (IAB)&quot;">
  <data key="d4">9.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"IAB operates under ISOC as its technical oversight body for internet protocol development, indicating a hierarchical organizational relationship."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-c4a2f7654873f1e1c6f37434762f36ae</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;INTERNET ARCHITECTURE BOARD (IAB)&quot;" target="&quot;INTERNET ENGINEERING TASK FORCE (IETF)&quot;">
  <data key="d4">10.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"IETF is a subordinate engineering body under IAB responsible for protocol standardization, showing a direct structural and functional relationship."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-c4a2f7654873f1e1c6f37434762f36ae</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;INTERNET ARCHITECTURE BOARD (IAB)&quot;" target="&quot;INTERNET RESEARCH TASK FORCE (IRTF)&quot;">
  <data key="d4">10.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"IRTF is a research-focused division under IAB, indicating a direct organizational and functional relationship."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-c4a2f7654873f1e1c6f37434762f36ae</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;INTERNET ENGINEERING TASK FORCE (IETF)&quot;" target="&quot;INTERNET ENGINEERING STEERING GROUP (IESG)&quot;">
  <data key="d4">10.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"The IESG manages the IETF’s activities and provides leadership over its working groups, reflecting a direct governance relationship."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-c4a2f7654873f1e1c6f37434762f36ae</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;INTERNET ENGINEERING TASK FORCE (IETF)&quot;" target="&quot;WORKING GROUP (WG)&quot;">
  <data key="d4">10.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Working Groups are the operational units of the IETF, formed to address specific protocol and standardization tasks, indicating a structural and functional dependency."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-c4a2f7654873f1e1c6f37434762f36ae</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;INTERNET RESEARCH TASK FORCE (IRTF)&quot;" target="&quot;INTERNET RESEARCH STEERING GROUP (IRSG)&quot;">
  <data key="d4">9.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"The IRSG manages the IRTF, guiding its research groups in studying long-term internet-related topics."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-e7c449009dbc73e669666df32bd7aeb1</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;WORLD WIDE WEB (WWW)&quot;" target="&quot;INTERNET STANDARDIZATION&quot;">
  <data key="d4">7.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"The widespread adoption of the WWW increased the need for standardized internet protocols, linking the WWW event to the broader standardization effort."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-c4a2f7654873f1e1c6f37434762f36ae</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;INTERNET ENGINEERING STEERING GROUP (IESG)&quot;" target="&quot;RFC (REQUEST FOR COMMENTS)&quot;">
  <data key="d4">8.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"The IESG oversees the engineering work that leads to the creation of RFC documents, particularly those progressing through the Proposed Standard to Internet Standard stages."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-e7c449009dbc73e669666df32bd7aeb1</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;INTERNET ENGINEERING STEERING GROUP (IESG)&quot;" target="&quot;WORKING GROUP (WG)&quot;">
  <data key="d4">9.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Working Groups operate under the management and oversight of the IESG, which coordinates their engineering efforts."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-e7c449009dbc73e669666df32bd7aeb1</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;WORKING GROUP (WG)&quot;" target="&quot;IETF FORUM&quot;">
  <data key="d4">8.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"The IETF Forum is composed of numerous Working Groups, each addressing specific engineering challenges."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-e7c449009dbc73e669666df32bd7aeb1</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;INTERNET RESEARCH STEERING GROUP (IRSG)&quot;" target="&quot;RESEARCH GROUP (RG)&quot;">
  <data key="d4">9.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Research Groups are managed by the IRSG, which provides strategic direction for long-term internet research."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-e7c449009dbc73e669666df32bd7aeb1</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;RFC (REQUEST FOR COMMENTS)&quot;" target="&quot;INTERNET DRAFT&quot;">
  <data key="d4">7.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"An Internet Draft precedes RFC status and may evolve into an RFC if it advances to the Proposed Standard stage."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-e7c449009dbc73e669666df32bd7aeb1</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;RFC (REQUEST FOR COMMENTS)&quot;" target="&quot;PROPOSED STANDARD&quot;">
  <data key="d4">9.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Documents designated as Proposed Standards are published as RFCs, marking their formal entry into the standards track."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-e7c449009dbc73e669666df32bd7aeb1</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;RFC (REQUEST FOR COMMENTS)&quot;" target="&quot;INTERNET STANDARD&quot;">
  <data key="d4">8.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Only a select few RFC documents eventually attain Internet Standard status, indicated by an STD number."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-e7c449009dbc73e669666df32bd7aeb1</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;RFC (REQUEST FOR COMMENTS)&quot;" target="&quot;INTERNET&quot;">
  <data key="d4">7.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"RFCs are published openly on the Internet, embodying its public and transparent standardization culture."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-e7c449009dbc73e669666df32bd7aeb1</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;RFC (REQUEST FOR COMMENTS)&quot;" target="&quot;HISTORICAL RFC&quot;">
  <data key="d4">6.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Historical RFCs constitute one of the five categories of RFC documents, retained for archival purposes within the broader RFC series."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-e7c449009dbc73e669666df32bd7aeb1</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;RFC (REQUEST FOR COMMENTS)&quot;" target="&quot;EXPERIMENTAL RFC&quot;">
  <data key="d4">6.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Experimental RFCs are a subclass of RFC documents designated for testing and not for production use."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-e7c449009dbc73e669666df32bd7aeb1</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;RFC (REQUEST FOR COMMENTS)&quot;" target="&quot;INFORMATIONAL RFC&quot;">
  <data key="d4">6.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Informational RFCs form a category within the RFC series that conveys non-standard-related knowledge about the Internet."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-e7c449009dbc73e669666df32bd7aeb1</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;RFC (REQUEST FOR COMMENTS)&quot;" target="&quot;RFC INDEX&quot;">
  <data key="d4">8.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"The RFC Index serves as the official catalog and navigational tool for the entire RFC document collection."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-e7c449009dbc73e669666df32bd7aeb1</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;PROPOSED STANDARD&quot;" target="&quot;INTERNET STANDARD&quot;">
  <data key="d4">10.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"A Proposed Standard can become an Internet Standard after extensive testing and validation over time."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-e7c449009dbc73e669666df32bd7aeb1</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;INTERNET STANDARD&quot;" target="&quot;RFC 6410&quot;">
  <data key="d4">8.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"RFC 6410 reformed the path to Internet Standard status by removing the Draft Standard phase, simplifying the process to Proposed Standard → Internet Standard."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-e7c449009dbc73e669666df32bd7aeb1</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;INTERNET STANDARD&quot;" target="&quot;STD92&quot;">
  <data key="d4">9.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"STD92 is an instance of an Internet Standard, representing the cumulative outcome of the standardization process as of late 2019."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-e7c449009dbc73e669666df32bd7aeb1</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;GOOGLE&quot;" target="&quot;YANGQUAN&quot;">
  <data key="d4">0.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"There is no direct relationship between Google and Yangquan mentioned in the text."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-30cc6309bbf7dc040e88d043d89a8722</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;GOOGLE&quot;" target="&quot;INTERNET&quot;">
  <data key="d4">9.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Google operates massive data centers that form critical parts of the Internet's edge infrastructure, providing services like search and video streaming over the Internet."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-30cc6309bbf7dc040e88d043d89a8722</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;BAIDU&quot;" target="&quot;YANGQUAN&quot;">
  <data key="d4">9.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Baidu built and operates a major data center in Yangquan, establishing a strong geographical and operational link."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-30cc6309bbf7dc040e88d043d89a8722</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;BAIDU&quot;" target="&quot;INTERNET&quot;">
  <data key="d4">9.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Baidu contributes to the Internet's edge through its large-scale data center, delivering online services to users via the global network."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-30cc6309bbf7dc040e88d043d89a8722</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;YANGQUAN&quot;" target="&quot;INTERNET&quot;">
  <data key="d4">7.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Yangquan hosts a major Baidu data center that supports Internet services, linking the geographic location to the global Internet infrastructure."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-30cc6309bbf7dc040e88d043d89a8722</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;SERVER&quot;" target="&quot;CLIENT&quot;">
  <data key="d4">9.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"In the client-server model, the client actively requests services while the server passively provides them; they engage in bidirectional communication after a connection is established."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-7076395e2a9a9ea6cdf6d289a0503201</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;SERVER&quot;" target="&quot;PEER-TO-PEER (P2P)&quot;">
  <data key="d4">8.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"In P2P architecture, each peer also functions as a server when providing data or services to other peers, embodying dual roles within the same system."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-7076395e2a9a9ea6cdf6d289a0503201</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;SERVER&quot;" target="&quot;FIGURE 1-5&quot;">
  <data key="d4">7.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Figure 1-5 visually depicts the server entity (as implemented on Host B) in the standard client-server interaction."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-7076395e2a9a9ea6cdf6d289a0503201</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;SCHOOL&quot;" target="&quot;INTERNET&quot;">
  <data key="d4">6.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Schools connect end systems to the Internet for communication and resource sharing, functioning as institutional users of Internet services."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-30cc6309bbf7dc040e88d043d89a8722</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;GOVERNMENT AGENCY&quot;" target="&quot;INTERNET&quot;">
  <data key="d4">6.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Government agencies connect hosts to the Internet, participating in the global network as institutional users."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-30cc6309bbf7dc040e88d043d89a8722</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;CLIENT&quot;" target="&quot;PEER-TO-PEER (P2P)&quot;">
  <data key="d4">8.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"In P2P architecture, each peer functions as both a client and a server depending on the interaction context—for example, when requesting data it acts as a client."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-7076395e2a9a9ea6cdf6d289a0503201</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;CLIENT&quot;" target="&quot;FIGURE 1-5&quot;">
  <data key="d4">7.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Figure 1-5 visually represents the client entity (as implemented on Host A) within the client-server communication model."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-7076395e2a9a9ea6cdf6d289a0503201</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;PEER-TO-PEER (P2P)&quot;" target="&quot;FIGURE 1-6&quot;">
  <data key="d4">9.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Figure 1-6 illustrates the P2P concept through multiple interconnected hosts, serving as a visual embodiment of the P2P communication event."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-7076395e2a9a9ea6cdf6d289a0503201</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;HOST C&quot;" target="&quot;HOST D&quot;">
  <data key="d4">8.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Host C and Host D engage in peer-to-peer communication, where either can act as client or server depending on the direction of the service request."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-7076395e2a9a9ea6cdf6d289a0503201</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;HOST C&quot;" target="&quot;HOST F&quot;">
  <data key="d4">8.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Host C and Host F are connected via P2P communication, enabling mutual data sharing as illustrated in Figure 1-6."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-7076395e2a9a9ea6cdf6d289a0503201</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;HOST C&quot;" target="&quot;PEER-TO-PEER COMMUNICATION&quot;">
  <data key="d4">9.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Host C actively participates in Peer-to-Peer Communication, exemplifying the fluid client-server duality inherent in P2P systems."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-7076395e2a9a9ea6cdf6d289a0503201</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;HOST D&quot;" target="&quot;PEER-TO-PEER COMMUNICATION&quot;">
  <data key="d4">9.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Host D is engaged in Peer-to-Peer Communication, interacting symmetrically with other peers like C and F."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-7076395e2a9a9ea6cdf6d289a0503201</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;HOST E&quot;" target="&quot;HOST F&quot;">
  <data key="d4">8.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Host E and Host F participate in symmetric P2P communication, exchanging services as equals."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-7076395e2a9a9ea6cdf6d289a0503201</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;HOST E&quot;" target="&quot;PEER-TO-PEER COMMUNICATION&quot;">
  <data key="d4">9.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Host E takes part in Peer-to-Peer Communication, sharing resources directly with other network peers."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-7076395e2a9a9ea6cdf6d289a0503201</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;HOST F&quot;" target="&quot;PEER-TO-PEER COMMUNICATION&quot;">
  <data key="d4">9.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Host F is a full participant in Peer-to-Peer Communication, functioning as both provider and requester of services."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-7076395e2a9a9ea6cdf6d289a0503201</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;TELEPHONE EXCHANGE&quot;" target="&quot;CIRCUIT SWITCHING&quot;">
  <data key="d4">10.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Telephone exchanges implement circuit switching by establishing, maintaining, and releasing dedicated physical connections between callers."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-eaddc23a827beb1a3d74ea7736adb0dc</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;TELEPHONE EXCHANGE&quot;" target="&quot;TRUNK LINE&quot;">
  <data key="d4">9.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Exchanges are interconnected via trunk lines, which carry multiple simultaneous calls using shared digital channels."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-eaddc23a827beb1a3d74ea7736adb0dc</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;CIRCUIT SWITCHING&quot;" target="&quot;TELECOMMUNICATION NETWORK&quot;">
  <data key="d4">9.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"The telecommunications network relies on circuit switching to manage telephone calls by dynamically allocating dedicated physical paths through interconnected switches."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-eaddc23a827beb1a3d74ea7736adb0dc</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;CIRCUIT SWITCHING&quot;" target="&quot;PACKET SWITCHING&quot;">
  <data key="d4">16.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Circuit switching and packet switching are contrasted as two distinct methods of resource allocation in communication networks; circuit switching dedicates resources for the entire call duration, while packet switching shares resources dynamically."&lt;SEP&gt;"Packet switching contrasts with circuit switching by not requiring a pre-established connection and instead dynamically allocating bandwidth per packet, improving efficiency for bursty traffic."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-1dbda231b683f35c9fc91e9cc64fa2b9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaddc23a827beb1a3d74ea7736adb0dc</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;CIRCUIT SWITCHING&quot;" target="&quot;CALL SETUP&quot;">
  <data key="d4">10.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Call setup is the initial phase of circuit switching, required before any voice communication can occur."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-eaddc23a827beb1a3d74ea7736adb0dc</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;CIRCUIT SWITCHING&quot;" target="&quot;CALL TEARDOWN&quot;">
  <data key="d4">10.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Call teardown is the final phase of circuit switching, freeing network resources after communication ends."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-eaddc23a827beb1a3d74ea7736adb0dc</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;PACKET SWITCHING&quot;" target="&quot;PACKET&quot;">
  <data key="d4">19.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Packets are the data units processed during packet switching; the entire mechanism revolves around their transmission."&lt;SEP&gt;"Packets are the fundamental data units processed and forwarded by the packet switching mechanism."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-b07dbbacc9a0b17e8854aaf7b97228e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaddc23a827beb1a3d74ea7736adb0dc</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;PACKET SWITCHING&quot;" target="&quot;INTERNET CORE&quot;">
  <data key="d4">10.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Packet switching is the operational paradigm of the Internet core, enabling dynamic bandwidth allocation, high reliability, and efficient handling of bursty data traffic."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-79ac7779be8905f2233724f344677601</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;PACKET SWITCHING&quot;" target="&quot;MESSAGE SWITCHING&quot;">
  <data key="d4">9.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Packet switching evolved from message switching by applying store-and-forward principles but using computerized processing and smaller units (packets), drastically reducing delay."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-1dbda231b683f35c9fc91e9cc64fa2b9</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;PACKET SWITCHING&quot;" target="&quot;POSTAL COMMUNICATION&quot;">
  <data key="d4">6.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Postal communication is presented as a historical conceptual precursor to packet switching due to its store-and-forward nature, though it lacks digital or real-time characteristics."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-1dbda231b683f35c9fc91e9cc64fa2b9</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;TELECOMMUNICATION NETWORK&quot;" target="&quot;BUSY SIGNAL&quot;">
  <data key="d4">8.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"The busy signal is generated by the telecommunications network when resource allocation during call setup fails due to congestion."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-eaddc23a827beb1a3d74ea7736adb0dc</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;LOCAL EXCHANGE&quot;" target="&quot;USER LINE&quot;">
  <data key="d4">9.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Each user line connects a single telephone subscriber directly to their local exchange, providing exclusive access to the network."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-eaddc23a827beb1a3d74ea7736adb0dc</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;MESSAGE&quot;" target="&quot;PACKET&quot;">
  <data key="d4">10.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"A message is divided into multiple packets for transmission in a packet-switched network; each packet carries part of the message along with routing metadata."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-eaddc23a827beb1a3d74ea7736adb0dc</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;ROUTER A&quot;" target="&quot;HOST H₁&quot;">
  <data key="d4">9.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Host H₁ sends data packets directly to Router A, establishing a direct communication link at the start of transmission."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-79ac7779be8905f2233724f344677601</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;ROUTER A&quot;" target="&quot;ROUTER C&quot;">
  <data key="d4">8.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Under normal conditions, Router A forwards packets to Router C as determined by its forwarding table."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-79ac7779be8905f2233724f344677601</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;ROUTER A&quot;" target="&quot;ROUTER B&quot;">
  <data key="d4">7.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"When link A–C is congested, Router A reroutes packets through Router B as an alternative path."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-79ac7779be8905f2233724f344677601</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;ROUTER B&quot;" target="&quot;ROUTER E&quot;">
  <data key="d4">9.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Router B forwards packets to Router E, serving both as an alternate path for H₁→H₅ and the primary path for H₂→H₆."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-79ac7779be8905f2233724f344677601</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;ROUTER B&quot;" target="&quot;HOST H₂&quot;">
  <data key="d4">8.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Host H₂ sends its packets to Router B to begin communication with Host H₆."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-79ac7779be8905f2233724f344677601</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;ROUTER C&quot;" target="&quot;ROUTER E&quot;">
  <data key="d4">8.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Router C forwards received packets to Router E en route to the final destination."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-79ac7779be8905f2233724f344677601</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;ROUTER E&quot;" target="&quot;HOST H₅&quot;">
  <data key="d4">10.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Router E delivers the final packets directly to Host H₅, completing the transmission."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-79ac7779be8905f2233724f344677601</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;ROUTER E&quot;" target="&quot;HOST H₆&quot;">
  <data key="d4">8.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Router E delivers packets from Host H₂ (via Router B) to Host H₆."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-79ac7779be8905f2233724f344677601</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;MESSAGE SWITCHING&quot;" target="&quot;TELEGRAPH COMMUNICATION&quot;">
  <data key="d4">10.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Telegraph communication in the 1940s is described as the practical implementation of message switching, using paper tapes and manual operator forwarding."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-1dbda231b683f35c9fc91e9cc64fa2b9</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;铁道部&quot;" target="&quot;CNPAC&quot;">
  <data key="d4">6.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"While the Ministry of Railways pioneered dedicated networks, CNPAC represented the first public packet-switched network, both contributing to China’s early networking landscape."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-779592fbef47ea27eab563c9f2b5fb8a</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;CNPAC&quot;" target="&quot;公安&quot;">
  <data key="d4">5.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Public security departments built their own networks around the same time CNPAC was launched, reflecting parallel developments in public and government networking."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-779592fbef47ea27eab563c9f2b5fb8a</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;CNPAC&quot;" target="&quot;军队&quot;">
  <data key="d4">5.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"The military established its own wide-area networks in the late 1980s, alongside the emergence of public infrastructure like CNPAC."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-779592fbef47ea27eab563c9f2b5fb8a</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;中国科学院高能物理研究所&quot;" target="&quot;中国第一个万维网服务器设立&quot;">
  <data key="d4">10.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"The Institute of High Energy Physics established China’s first WWW server in May 1994."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-779592fbef47ea27eab563c9f2b5fb8a</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;CHINANET&quot;" target="&quot;CHINANET正式启动&quot;">
  <data key="d4">10.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"CHINANET was officially launched in September 1994 as China’s first public computer internet."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-779592fbef47ea27eab563c9f2b5fb8a</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;CERNET2&quot;" target="&quot;CERNET2试验网开通&quot;">
  <data key="d4">10.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"CERNET2’s trial network was launched in February 2004, marking a milestone in China’s next-generation internet development."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-779592fbef47ea27eab563c9f2b5fb8a</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;CERNET2&quot;" target="&quot;北京&quot;">
  <data key="d4">9.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Beijing is a core node connected by the CERNET2 backbone network."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-779592fbef47ea27eab563c9f2b5fb8a</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;CERNET2&quot;" target="&quot;上海&quot;">
  <data key="d4">9.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Shanghai is a core node connected by the CERNET2 backbone network."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-779592fbef47ea27eab563c9f2b5fb8a</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;CERNET2&quot;" target="&quot;广州&quot;">
  <data key="d4">9.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Guangzhou is a core node connected by the CERNET2 backbone network."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-779592fbef47ea27eab563c9f2b5fb8a</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;中国互联网络信息中心 CNNIC&quot;" target="&quot;中国正式连入互联网&quot;">
  <data key="d4">7.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"CNNIC tracks internet development in China, which began with the official connection on April 20, 1994."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-779592fbef47ea27eab563c9f2b5fb8a</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;张朝阳&quot;" target="&quot;爱特信公司&quot;">
  <data key="d4">10.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Zhang Chaoyang founded Aitexin Company in 1996."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-779592fbef47ea27eab563c9f2b5fb8a</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;爱特信公司&quot;" target="&quot;搜狐公司&quot;">
  <data key="d4">20.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Aitexin Company was renamed Sohu Inc. after launching the 'Sohu' product, marking a strategic rebranding."&lt;SEP&gt;"Aitexin Company was renamed Sohu Inc. after launching the Sohu portal in 1998."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-779592fbef47ea27eab563c9f2b5fb8a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3cf7f9315ae398e3c73bd500746d26d8</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;爱特信公司&quot;" target="&quot;阳&quot;">
  <data key="d4">10.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Yang founded Aitexin Company, establishing China's first venture capital-backed internet firm."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-3cf7f9315ae398e3c73bd500746d26d8</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;搜狐公司&quot;" target="&quot;搜狐网站&quot;">
  <data key="d4">18.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Sohu Inc.'s main product is Sohu.com, which serves as its flagship platform and core business asset."&lt;SEP&gt;"Sohu Inc.’s main product is Sohu.com, China’s first large-scale categorized search engine and later a comprehensive portal."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-779592fbef47ea27eab563c9f2b5fb8a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3cf7f9315ae398e3c73bd500746d26d8</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;丁磊&quot;" target="&quot;网易公司&quot;">
  <data key="d4">20.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Ding Lei founded NetEase (Net) in 1997."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ding Lei founded NetEase and led the development of its key products including China’s first Chinese full-text search engine and popular email services."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-779592fbef47ea27eab563c9f2b5fb8a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3cf7f9315ae398e3c73bd500746d26d8</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;网易公司&quot;" target="&quot;网易网站&quot;">
  <data key="d4">9.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"NetEase operates NetEase website as a nationally recognized comprehensive portal, central to its business identity."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-3cf7f9315ae398e3c73bd500746d26d8</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;微信&quot;" target="&quot;腾讯公司&quot;">
  <data key="d4">10.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Tencent launched WeChat in 2011, a transformative product that expanded its ecosystem into mobile communication and digital payments."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-3cf7f9315ae398e3c73bd500746d26d8</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;微信&quot;" target="&quot;张小龙&quot;">
  <data key="d4">9.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Zhang Xiaolong led the successful development of WeChat at Tencent, building on his prior experience with Foxmail."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-3cf7f9315ae398e3c73bd500746d26d8</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;王志东&quot;" target="&quot;新浪网站&quot;">
  <data key="d4">10.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Wang Zhidong founded Sina.com, which grew under his leadership into the world’s largest Chinese comprehensive portal."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-3cf7f9315ae398e3c73bd500746d26d8</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;马化腾&quot;" target="&quot;腾讯公司&quot;">
  <data key="d4">10.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Ma Huateng co-founded Tencent and has been instrumental in its growth into a leading internet service provider through products like QQ and WeChat."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-3cf7f9315ae398e3c73bd500746d26d8</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;张志东&quot;" target="&quot;腾讯公司&quot;">
  <data key="d4">9.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Zhang Zhidong co-founded Tencent alongside Ma Huateng, playing a foundational role in the company’s establishment."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-3cf7f9315ae398e3c73bd500746d26d8</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;腾讯公司&quot;" target="&quot;OICQ/QQ&quot;">
  <data key="d4">10.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Tencent developed and launched OICQ (later QQ) in 1999 as its first major product, which became ubiquitous among Chinese internet users."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-3cf7f9315ae398e3c73bd500746d26d8</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;李彦宏&quot;" target="&quot;百度网站&quot;">
  <data key="d4">10.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Li Yanhong co-founded and built Baidu.com into China’s dominant search engine, especially after Google’s exit from China."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-3cf7f9315ae398e3c73bd500746d26d8</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;徐勇&quot;" target="&quot;百度网站&quot;">
  <data key="d4">9.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Xu Yong co-founded Baidu.com with Li Yanhong, contributing to its establishment as a major search platform."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-3cf7f9315ae398e3c73bd500746d26d8</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;马云&quot;" target="&quot;阿里巴巴网站&quot;">
  <data key="d4">10.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Jack Ma founded Alibaba.com as a B2B e-commerce platform, laying the foundation for his broader e-commerce ecosystem."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-3cf7f9315ae398e3c73bd500746d26d8</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;马云&quot;" target="&quot;淘宝网&quot;">
  <data key="d4">10.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Jack Ma launched Taobao.com in 2003 as a C2C marketplace under Alibaba Group, expanding his e-commerce reach."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-3cf7f9315ae398e3c73bd500746d26d8</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;支付宝&quot;" target="&quot;阿里巴巴集团&quot;">
  <data key="d4">10.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Alibaba Group created Alipay in 2004 as a secure third-party payment solution to support its e-commerce platforms."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-3cf7f9315ae398e3c73bd500746d26d8</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;TELECOMMUNICATION COMPANIES&quot;" target="&quot;WIDE AREA NETWORK (WAN)&quot;">
  <data key="d4">8.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Telecommunication companies likely operate or provide infrastructure for wide area networks, which form the backbone of public internet connectivity."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-982f03b0a500a9e6bd7272aaa9e050d8</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;TELECOMMUNICATION COMPANIES&quot;" target="&quot;PUBLIC NETWORK&quot;">
  <data key="d4">10.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Telecommunication companies build and operate public networks, establishing direct ownership and service provision."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-982f03b0a500a9e6bd7272aaa9e050d8</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;MILITARY&quot;" target="&quot;PRIVATE NETWORK&quot;">
  <data key="d4">18.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"The military is explicitly cited as an organization that builds and uses its own private network for special operational needs."&lt;SEP&gt;"The military uses a private network for its exclusive communications, indicating a strong ownership and usage relationship."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-982f03b0a500a9e6bd7272aaa9e050d8</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;RAILWAY SYSTEM&quot;" target="&quot;PRIVATE NETWORK&quot;">
  <data key="d4">18.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"The railway system is listed as an example of an organization maintaining its own private network for internal operations."&lt;SEP&gt;"The railway system relies on its own private network to fulfill specialized operational needs, showing a clear organizational dependency."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-982f03b0a500a9e6bd7272aaa9e050d8</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;BANKING SYSTEM&quot;" target="&quot;PRIVATE NETWORK&quot;">
  <data key="d4">18.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Banks are described as operating private networks to support their unique business requirements."&lt;SEP&gt;"Banks depend on private networks for secure internal operations, reflecting a tight integration between the organization and its network."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-982f03b0a500a9e6bd7272aaa9e050d8</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;POWER SYSTEM&quot;" target="&quot;PRIVATE NETWORK&quot;">
  <data key="d4">18.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"The power sector is noted as having its own private network, used exclusively for internal purposes."&lt;SEP&gt;"The power sector utilizes a private network exclusively for internal functions, demonstrating a strong operational link."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-982f03b0a500a9e6bd7272aaa9e050d8</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;WIDE AREA NETWORK (WAN)&quot;" target="&quot;INTERNET&quot;">
  <data key="d4">10.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Wide Area Networks constitute the core of the internet, responsible for long-distance data transport across countries."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-982f03b0a500a9e6bd7272aaa9e050d8</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;METROPOLITAN AREA NETWORK (MAN)&quot;" target="&quot;LOCAL AREA NETWORK (LAN)&quot;">
  <data key="d4">8.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Metropolitan Area Networks interconnect multiple Local Area Networks across a city, establishing a hierarchical and functional relationship."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-982f03b0a500a9e6bd7272aaa9e050d8</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;LOCAL AREA NETWORK (LAN)&quot;" target="&quot;ACCESS NETWORK (AN)&quot;">
  <data key="d4">7.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Many access networks are classified as local area networks, indicating a categorical and structural overlap between these two geo entities."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-982f03b0a500a9e6bd7272aaa9e050d8</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;LOCAL AREA NETWORK (LAN)&quot;" target="&quot;CAMPUS NETWORK&quot;">
  <data key="d4">9.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"A campus network is explicitly defined as multiple interconnected LANs within a school, showing a compositional relationship."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-982f03b0a500a9e6bd7272aaa9e050d8</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;LOCAL AREA NETWORK (LAN)&quot;" target="&quot;ENTERPRISE NETWORK&quot;">
  <data key="d4">9.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"An enterprise network is described as many interconnected LANs within a company, indicating a structural dependency."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-982f03b0a500a9e6bd7272aaa9e050d8</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;ACCESS NETWORK (AN)&quot;" target="&quot;INTERNET&quot;">
  <data key="d4">8.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"The access network serves as the bridge between user devices and the internet, connecting them to the broader global infrastructure."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-982f03b0a500a9e6bd7272aaa9e050d8</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;PUBLIC NETWORK&quot;" target="&quot;INTERNET&quot;">
  <data key="d4">7.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Public networks can carry computer data and form part of the broader internet infrastructure when used for public internet services."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-982f03b0a500a9e6bd7272aaa9e050d8</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;PRIVATE NETWORK&quot;" target="&quot;INTERNET&quot;">
  <data key="d4">5.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Private networks may be separate from the internet but can also connect to it; however, the text implies they are primarily isolated, so the relationship is weak but exists in the context of classification."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-982f03b0a500a9e6bd7272aaa9e050d8</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;BIT&quot;" target="&quot;DATA RATE&quot;">
  <data key="d4">9.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Bit is the base unit used to express data rate, which measures how many bits are transferred per second."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-a2ab20554e89073ba513e052dbf5667c</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;DATA RATE&quot;" target="&quot;BANDWIDTH&quot;">
  <data key="d4">10.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"In networking contexts, bandwidth defines the maximum possible data rate of a communication channel."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-a2ab20554e89073ba513e052dbf5667c</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;BANDWIDTH&quot;" target="&quot;THROUGHPUT&quot;">
  <data key="d4">9.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Throughput is constrained by available bandwidth and cannot exceed the channel's nominal data rate."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-a2ab20554e89073ba513e052dbf5667c</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;SERVER B&quot;" target="&quot;INTERNET&quot;">
  <data key="d4">7.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Server B connects to and delivers data through the Internet to multiple clients simultaneously."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-a2ab20554e89073ba513e052dbf5667c</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;主机 A&quot;" target="&quot;主机 B&quot;">
  <data key="d4">9.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"主机 A sends data or files directly to 主机 B across various network topologies described in multiple problems."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-c24d88fe5e8a41451cb6e5c8bf5bb54c</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;主机 A&quot;" target="&quot;节点交换机&quot;">
  <data key="d4">8.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"主机 A transmits data first to a 节点交换机, which then forwards it toward 主机 B in both message and packet switching scenarios."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-c24d88fe5e8a41451cb6e5c8bf5bb54c</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;主机 A&quot;" target="&quot;报文交换实验&quot;">
  <data key="d4">9.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"主机 A is the sender in the 报文交换实验, initiating transmission of an undivided message."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-c24d88fe5e8a41451cb6e5c8bf5bb54c</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;主机 A&quot;" target="&quot;分组交换实验&quot;">
  <data key="d4">9.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"主机 A participates in the 分组交换实验 by segmenting and sending 1000 packets to 主机 B via intermediate switches."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-c24d88fe5e8a41451cb6e5c8bf5bb54c</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;主机 A&quot;" target="&quot;吞吐量计算事件&quot;">
  <data key="d4">9.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"主机 A sends a 10 MB file in the 吞吐量计算事件 across a heterogeneous link path to 主机 B."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-c24d88fe5e8a41451cb6e5c8bf5bb54c</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;主机 A&quot;" target="&quot;点对点链路&quot;">
  <data key="d4">7.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"主机 A uses the 点对点链路 (e.g., 20000 km long) as the physical medium for sending data in several problems."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-c24d88fe5e8a41451cb6e5c8bf5bb54c</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;主机 A&quot;" target="&quot;20000 KM 链路&quot;">
  <data key="d4">8.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"主机 A transmits data over the 20000 KM 链路 in problems 1-30 and 1-31."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-c24d88fe5e8a41451cb6e5c8bf5bb54c</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;主机 A&quot;" target="&quot;5000 KM 链路&quot;">
  <data key="d4">9.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"主机 A sends a 600,000-bit file across the 5000 KM 链路 in problem 1-35."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-c24d88fe5e8a41451cb6e5c8bf5bb54c</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;主机 A&quot;" target="&quot;三段链路路径&quot;">
  <data key="d4">9.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"主机 A initiates file transfer through the 三段链路路径 in problem 1-36."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-c24d88fe5e8a41451cb6e5c8bf5bb54c</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;路由器&quot;" target="&quot;排队时延溢出事件&quot;">
  <data key="d4">8.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Routers generate queuing delay, and under high traffic, their queues may overflow, triggering the packet loss event described."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-0174bb3213f0a9872650fbd9b001dae3</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;路由器&quot;" target="&quot;主机1&quot;">
  <data key="d4">8.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Data originating from Host 1 is physically transmitted across the network to the router, which processes and forwards it toward Host 2."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-d13275eb4af2ef9a790f16e7564a101b</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;路由器&quot;" target="&quot;主机2&quot;">
  <data key="d4">8.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"The router forwards the processed data toward Host 2, which receives the bitstream and begins processing it upward through its protocol layers."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-d13275eb4af2ef9a790f16e7564a101b</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;路由器&quot;" target="&quot;OSI参考模型&quot;">
  <data key="d4">7.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"The router implements functionality aligned with the lower layers (particularly Layer 3) of the OSI reference model to route data between hosts."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-d13275eb4af2ef9a790f16e7564a101b</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;光纤&quot;" target="&quot;传播时延&quot;">
  <data key="d4">8.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Although '传播时延' itself isn't an extracted entity, the text explicitly ties fiber optics to propagation delay (e.g., 1000 km of fiber yields ~5 ms delay), so this relationship captures that causal link via the '总时延计算' event context."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-0174bb3213f0a9872650fbd9b001dae3</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;收费站比喻事件&quot;" target="&quot;发送时延&quot;">
  <data key="d4">9.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"The tollbooth analogy models sending delay as the time to process all cars through the booth (60 seconds for 10 cars), directly mapping to transmission delay in networking."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-0174bb3213f0a9872650fbd9b001dae3</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;收费站比喻事件&quot;" target="&quot;传播时延&quot;">
  <data key="d4">9.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"In the analogy, the 30-minute drive over 50 km represents propagation delay, clearly linking distance and travel speed to this network concept."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-0174bb3213f0a9872650fbd9b001dae3</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;总时延计算&quot;" target="&quot;处理时延&quot;">
  <data key="d4">8.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Processing delay is one of the four additive components in the total delay equation, reflecting time spent by hosts or routers analyzing packet headers and making forwarding decisions."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-0174bb3213f0a9872650fbd9b001dae3</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;IBM&quot;" target="&quot;SYSTEM NETWORK ARCHITECTURE (SNA)&quot;">
  <data key="d4">10.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"IBM developed and announced the System Network Architecture (SNA) in 1974 as its proprietary layered network standard."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-8fe573a1edca72839801eaf0704a5d62</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATION FOR STANDARDIZATION (ISO)&quot;" target="&quot;OPEN SYSTEMS INTERCONNECTION REFERENCE MODEL (OSI/RM)&quot;">
  <data key="d4">10.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"ISO created the OSI/RM as an international standard framework to enable interoperability among different computer networks, formalized in 1983 as ISO 7498."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-8fe573a1edca72839801eaf0704a5d62</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;ISO&quot;" target="&quot;OSI&quot;">
  <data key="d4">9.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"ISO developed the formal international standard ISO 7498, which defines the OSI seven-layer reference model, making it the primary organizational driver behind the OSI initiative."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-7d24b4f467e6a4552877bf9e42c614b2</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;ISO&quot;" target="&quot;CCITT&quot;">
  <data key="d4">7.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"ISO and CCITT jointly developed OSI standards as their domains—information processing and telecommunications—converged, reflecting institutional cooperation in response to technological integration."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-7d24b4f467e6a4552877bf9e42c614b2</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;CCITT&quot;" target="&quot;OSI&quot;">
  <data key="d4">8.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"CCITT co-developed key OSI-related standards with ISO, notably the X.200 recommendation, which is functionally equivalent to ISO 7498, showing close collaboration on the OSI framework."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-7d24b4f467e6a4552877bf9e42c614b2</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;OSI&quot;" target="&quot;OSI SEVEN-LAYER MODEL&quot;">
  <data key="d4">9.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"OSI developed or is associated with the seven-layer reference model for network communication."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-e05b6bd70dc7a04d2758501d65696eff</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;主机 1&quot;" target="&quot;主机 2&quot;">
  <data key="d4">9.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Host 1 and Host 2 are directly connected through a communication network for the purpose of transferring files, utilizing layered protocol modules including file transfer, communication service, and network access layers."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-a1b5e11d873f879fd9610fda65292066</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;主机 1&quot;" target="&quot;文件传送模块&quot;">
  <data key="d4">8.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"The file transfer module runs on Host 1 to manage outgoing file transfers, making it an integral functional component of Host 1's network stack."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-a1b5e11d873f879fd9610fda65292066</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;主机 2&quot;" target="&quot;文件传送模块&quot;">
  <data key="d4">8.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"The file transfer module also operates on Host 2 to receive and store incoming files, establishing a peer-to-peer logical connection between the two hosts."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-a1b5e11d873f879fd9610fda65292066</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;文件传送模块&quot;" target="&quot;通信服务模块&quot;">
  <data key="d4">9.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"The file transfer module relies on the communication service module to handle reliable data exchange, demonstrating a clear client-service relationship in the protocol hierarchy."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-a1b5e11d873f879fd9610fda65292066</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;通信服务模块&quot;" target="&quot;网络接入模块&quot;">
  <data key="d4">9.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"The communication service module depends on the network access module to manage physical network interface details, forming a layered dependency in the protocol stack."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-a1b5e11d873f879fd9610fda65292066</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;通信服务模块&quot;" target="&quot;电子邮件模块&quot;">
  <data key="d4">7.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"The email module, like the file transfer module, uses the communication service module for reliable communication, indicating a reusable service relationship."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-a1b5e11d873f879fd9610fda65292066</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;IEEE 802 COMMITTEE&quot;" target="&quot;LINK LAYER&quot;">
  <data key="d4">18.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"The IEEE 802 Committee defines the link-layer (or media access control) standards used in TCP/IP implementations, which are outside the scope of IETF's work."&lt;SEP&gt;"The IEEE 802 Committee defines the technical standards that implement the Link Layer in real-world networks."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-e05b6bd70dc7a04d2758501d65696eff</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;IEEE 802 COMMITTEE&quot;" target="&quot;IEEE&quot;">
  <data key="d4">10.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"The IEEE 802 Committee operates under the IEEE umbrella to develop data link and physical layer standards for local networks."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-e05b6bd70dc7a04d2758501d65696eff</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;FIVE-LAYER PROTOCOL ARCHITECTURE&quot;" target="&quot;OSI SEVEN-LAYER MODEL&quot;">
  <data key="d4">7.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"The five-layer model integrates concepts from the OSI seven-layer model for pedagogical clarity."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-e05b6bd70dc7a04d2758501d65696eff</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;FIVE-LAYER PROTOCOL ARCHITECTURE&quot;" target="&quot;TCP/IP FOUR-LAYER MODEL&quot;">
  <data key="d4">7.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"The five-layer model also incorporates elements of the TCP/IP four-layer model to balance theory and practice in teaching."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-e05b6bd70dc7a04d2758501d65696eff</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;FIVE-LAYER PROTOCOL ARCHITECTURE&quot;" target="&quot;APPLICATION LAYER&quot;">
  <data key="d4">7.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"The Application Layer is one of the five layers in the hybrid educational model used to teach networking principles."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-e05b6bd70dc7a04d2758501d65696eff</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;FIVE-LAYER PROTOCOL ARCHITECTURE&quot;" target="&quot;TRANSPORT LAYER&quot;">
  <data key="d4">7.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"The Transport Layer is included in the five-layer model to handle process-to-process communication."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-e05b6bd70dc7a04d2758501d65696eff</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;FIVE-LAYER PROTOCOL ARCHITECTURE&quot;" target="&quot;INTERNET LAYER&quot;">
  <data key="d4">7.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"The Internet Layer in the five-layer model addresses inter-network communication and corresponds to the network layer in OSI."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-e05b6bd70dc7a04d2758501d65696eff</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;OSI SEVEN-LAYER MODEL&quot;" target="&quot;NETWORK ARCHITECTURE&quot;">
  <data key="d4">8.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"The OSI model is an instance of a network architecture that provides a theoretical framework for protocol layering."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-e05b6bd70dc7a04d2758501d65696eff</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;TCP/IP FOUR-LAYER MODEL&quot;" target="&quot;RFC 1122&quot;">
  <data key="d4">9.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"RFC 1122 formally documents the structure and requirements of the TCP/IP four-layer model, especially defining the link layer."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-e05b6bd70dc7a04d2758501d65696eff</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;TCP/IP FOUR-LAYER MODEL&quot;" target="&quot;NETWORK ARCHITECTURE&quot;">
  <data key="d4">9.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"The TCP/IP model is a practical implementation of a network architecture used on the Internet."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-e05b6bd70dc7a04d2758501d65696eff</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;RFC 1122&quot;" target="&quot;STD3&quot;">
  <data key="d4">10.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"STD3 is the standards track designation that includes RFC 1122 as a core specification for internet hosts."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-e05b6bd70dc7a04d2758501d65696eff</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;IP (INTERNET PROTOCOL)&quot;" target="&quot;TCP/IP PROTOCOL SUITE&quot;">
  <data key="d4">10.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"IP is a foundational component of the TCP/IP protocol suite, operating at the network (internetwork) layer."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-1c827098a559a559af3c8df103949b45</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;TCP/IP PROTOCOL SUITE&quot;" target="&quot;INTERNET&quot;">
  <data key="d4">10.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"The TCP/IP Protocol Suite provides the architectural and functional foundation that enables the Internet to achieve global scale and interoperability, with the IP protocol acting as the central unifying element."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-ad6543c9c55fc16571200afb61505ac0</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;TCP/IP PROTOCOL SUITE&quot;" target="&quot;IP PROTOCOL&quot;">
  <data key="d4">10.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"The IP Protocol serves as the core (narrow waist) of the TCP/IP Protocol Suite, around which the entire sandglass-shaped architecture is organized."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-ad6543c9c55fc16571200afb61505ac0</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;APPLICATION PROCESS AP₁&quot;" target="&quot;APPLICATION PROCESS AP₂&quot;">
  <data key="d4">9.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"AP₁ sends data to AP₂ through the layered protocol stack across the Internet, establishing a direct logical communication relationship."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-1c827098a559a559af3c8df103949b45</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;OSI参考模型&quot;" target="&quot;应用进程 AP₁&quot;">
  <data key="d4">8.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"The OSI reference model provides the layered framework within which AP₁ operates, defining how data is encapsulated and transmitted."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-d13275eb4af2ef9a790f16e7564a101b</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;OSI参考模型&quot;" target="&quot;应用进程 AP₂&quot;">
  <data key="d4">8.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"The OSI reference model defines the layered structure that AP₂ uses to decapsulate and interpret incoming data."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-d13275eb4af2ef9a790f16e7564a101b</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;OSI参考模型&quot;" target="&quot;协议栈&quot;">
  <data key="d4">8.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"The protocol stack is a practical implementation concept derived from the OSI reference model, representing the vertical arrangement of protocol layers."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-d13275eb4af2ef9a790f16e7564a101b</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;主机1&quot;" target="&quot;应用进程 AP₁&quot;">
  <data key="d4">9.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"AP₁ resides on and operates within Host 1, initiating the data transmission process from this source host."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-d13275eb4af2ef9a790f16e7564a101b</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;主机2&quot;" target="&quot;应用进程 AP₂&quot;">
  <data key="d4">9.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"AP₂ resides on and operates within Host 2, receiving data at the end of the transmission process on this destination host."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-d13275eb4af2ef9a790f16e7564a101b</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;应用进程 AP₁&quot;" target="&quot;应用进程 AP₂&quot;">
  <data key="d4">7.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Although they do not communicate directly, AP₁ and AP₂ are logically paired as sender and receiver in an end-to-end data exchange, abstracted by the layered protocol architecture."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-d13275eb4af2ef9a790f16e7564a101b</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;协议栈&quot;" target="&quot;实体&quot;">
  <data key="d4">7.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Entities reside within layers of the protocol stack and perform communication tasks according to the stack’s layered design."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-d13275eb4af2ef9a790f16e7564a101b</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;对等层&quot;" target="&quot;协议&quot;">
  <data key="d4">9.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Protocols define the rules that govern communication between peer layers, enabling them to exchange PDUs correctly."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-d13275eb4af2ef9a790f16e7564a101b</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;对等层&quot;" target="&quot;PDU&quot;">
  <data key="d4">9.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Peer layers exchange PDUs as their fundamental communication units, each PDU containing layer-specific control information and payload."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-d13275eb4af2ef9a790f16e7564a101b</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;实体&quot;" target="&quot;协议&quot;">
  <data key="d4">8.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Entities use protocols to communicate with their peers; protocols dictate how entities format and interpret messages."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-d13275eb4af2ef9a790f16e7564a101b</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;实体&quot;" target="&quot;服务&quot;">
  <data key="d4">8.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Entities in a given layer consume services provided by entities in the layer below through service access points."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-d13275eb4af2ef9a790f16e7564a101b</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;服务&quot;" target="&quot;服务访问点&quot;">
  <data key="d4">9.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Services are delivered to upper-layer entities via service access points, which act as the interface for inter-layer communication."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-d13275eb4af2ef9a790f16e7564a101b</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;服务&quot;" target="&quot;服务原语&quot;">
  <data key="d4">8.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Service primitives are the mechanism through which services are requested and delivered between adjacent layers in the OSI model."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-d13275eb4af2ef9a790f16e7564a101b</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;蓝军 1&quot;" target="&quot;蓝军 2&quot;">
  <data key="d4">18.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"蓝军 1 and 蓝军 2 are engaged in a mutual confirmation protocol where each waits for acknowledgment from the other, creating an infinite loop that prevents either from acting decisively."&lt;SEP&gt;"蓝军 1 and 蓝军 2 must coordinate their attack through unreliable communication channels, exchanging messages and confirmations to ensure synchronized action."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-77d64847854ffc2a5d57b6da6e7bc7d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9aad351f9f6f14230a142381a106309</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;蓝军 1&quot;" target="&quot;白军&quot;">
  <data key="d4">8.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"蓝军 1 is in direct conflict with 白军 and cannot defeat it alone, necessitating coordination with 蓝军 2."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-b9aad351f9f6f14230a142381a106309</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;蓝军 1&quot;" target="&quot;协同作战&quot;">
  <data key="d4">10.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"蓝军 1 initiates and participates in the 协同作战 event by proposing the attack time and seeking confirmation."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-b9aad351f9f6f14230a142381a106309</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;蓝军 2&quot;" target="&quot;白军&quot;">
  <data key="d4">8.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"蓝军 2 is in direct conflict with 白军 and cannot defeat it alone, requiring joint action with 蓝军 1."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-b9aad351f9f6f14230a142381a106309</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;蓝军 2&quot;" target="&quot;协同作战&quot;">
  <data key="d4">10.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"蓝军 2 is essential to the 协同作战 event, as its participation is required for victory, and it engages in message exchanges to confirm involvement."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-b9aad351f9f6f14230a142381a106309</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;CLIENT PROCESS&quot;" target="&quot;SERVER PROCESS&quot;">
  <data key="d4">18.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"Client Processes initiate requests to Server Processes in a client-server interaction, as illustrated in Figures 1-23 and 1-24, where communication occurs logically via horizontal virtual connections at the application layer."&lt;SEP&gt;"In the client-server model, client processes request services and server processes fulfill those requests, forming a fundamental interaction pattern in computer networks."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-d8da4662c1f51095b9efcbe2f2cbe556&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ad6543c9c55fc16571200afb61505ac0</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;IP PROTOCOL&quot;" target="&quot;INTERNET&quot;">
  <data key="d4">10.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"The IP Protocol is the foundational enabler of the Internet’s global scale and interoperability, implementing the 'IP over everything' and 'everything over IP' principles that allow heterogeneous networks and applications to interconnect seamlessly."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-ad6543c9c55fc16571200afb61505ac0</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;WAN&quot;" target="&quot;MAN&quot;">
  <data key="d4">6.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"WANs and MANs are both network categories differentiated by scale; MANs cover metropolitan areas while WANs span much larger regions, often interconnecting multiple MANs or LANs."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-d8da4662c1f51095b9efcbe2f2cbe556</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;LAN&quot;" target="&quot;PAN&quot;">
  <data key="d4">6.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"LANs and PANs are network types differing in coverage scope; PANs serve individual users’ devices, while LANs connect multiple devices within a local site."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-d8da4662c1f51095b9efcbe2f2cbe556</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;THREE-TIER ISP STRUCTURE&quot;" target="&quot;INTERNET&quot;">
  <data key="d4">8.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"The three-tier ISP structure forms the organizational and infrastructural backbone of the modern Internet, enabling global connectivity."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-d8da4662c1f51095b9efcbe2f2cbe556</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;主机 B&quot;" target="&quot;节点交换机&quot;">
  <data key="d4">8.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"节点交换机 forwards received data from 主机 A to 主机 B as part of multi-hop communication paths."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-c24d88fe5e8a41451cb6e5c8bf5bb54c</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;主机 B&quot;" target="&quot;点对点链路&quot;">
  <data key="d4">7.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"主机 B receives data over the 点对点链路 from 主机 A in various transmission scenarios."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-c24d88fe5e8a41451cb6e5c8bf5bb54c</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;主机 B&quot;" target="&quot;20000 KM 链路&quot;">
  <data key="d4">8.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"主机 B is the receiver at the far end of the 20000 KM 链路."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-c24d88fe5e8a41451cb6e5c8bf5bb54c</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;主机 B&quot;" target="&quot;5000 KM 链路&quot;">
  <data key="d4">9.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"主机 B receives the file sent over the 5000 KM 链路 from 主机 A."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-c24d88fe5e8a41451cb6e5c8bf5bb54c</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;主机 B&quot;" target="&quot;三段链路路径&quot;">
  <data key="d4">9.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"主机 B is the final destination after the 三段链路路径."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-c24d88fe5e8a41451cb6e5c8bf5bb54c</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;点对点链路&quot;" target="&quot;RTT 测量&quot;">
  <data key="d4">6.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"The 点对点链路 context provides the physical basis for discussing why RTT is relevant despite unidirectional data flow."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-c24d88fe5e8a41451cb6e5c8bf5bb54c</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;吞吐量计算事件&quot;" target="&quot;500 KBIT/S 链路&quot;">
  <data key="d4">10.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"The 500 KBIT/S 链路 is the bottleneck that determines throughput in the 吞吐量计算事件."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-c24d88fe5e8a41451cb6e5c8bf5bb54c</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;20000 KM 链路&quot;" target="&quot;比特可视化事件&quot;">
  <data key="d4">10.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"The 比特可视化事件 explicitly takes place on the 20000 KM 链路."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-c24d88fe5e8a41451cb6e5c8bf5bb54c</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;5000 KM 链路&quot;" target="&quot;带宽-传播综合分析事件&quot;">
  <data key="d4">10.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"The 带宽-传播综合分析事件 is conducted entirely on the 5000 KM 链路."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-c24d88fe5e8a41451cb6e5c8bf5bb54c</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;1 GBIT/S 链路&quot;" target="&quot;传播时延分析事件&quot;">
  <data key="d4">7.0</data>
  <data key="d5">"The 1 GBIT/S 链路 is central to computing bit width in problem 1-32, part of the broader 传播时延分析事件."</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-c24d88fe5e8a41451cb6e5c8bf5bb54c</data>
  <data key="d7">1</data>
</edge>
</graph></graphml>